{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Reflected XSS in /util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp HostName parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflected XSS in /util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp HostName parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `HostName` query parameter of `/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp` is written directly into an HTML response with no output encoding. Because the response is served with `Content-Type: text/html`, an attacker-supplied value containing markup is rendered and executed by the victim's browser, enabling reflected cross-site scripting on an unauthenticated endpoint."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `HostName` query parameter of `/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp` is written directly into an HTML response with no output encoding. Because the response is served with `Content-Type: text/html`, an attacker-supplied value containing markup is rendered and executed by the victim's browser, enabling reflected cross-site scripting on an unauthenticated endpoint.\n\nAn attacker who induces a victim to open a crafted link executes arbitrary JavaScript in the origin of the application. This permits theft of the victim's authenticated session material (the `JSESSIONID` and the non-HttpOnly `AltoroAccounts` cookie), manipulation of page content, forced state-changing requests in the victim's authenticated context, and credential/one-time-code phishing. No authentication is required to deliver the payload, and the endpoint is directly reachable over the network.\n\n1. HTML-encode the `HostName` value before it is written into the response, using the application's existing encoder `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb` (backed by `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) rather than the weaker regex-based `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`.\n2. Correct the response type: a JSON payload should be served as `application/json`, not `text/html`. This removes the browser's HTML parsing context for the response and is the appropriate fix for the endpoint's actual purpose.\n3. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (for example `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`) and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` at the application or container level as defense in depth.\n4. Apply the same server-side output encoding to all other locations that reflect user input (for example the feedback confirmation pages) and prefer the framework's implicit escaping over raw scriptlet output.",
                "markdown": "The `HostName` query parameter of `/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp` is written directly into an HTML response with no output encoding. Because the response is served with `Content-Type: text/html`, an attacker-supplied value containing markup is rendered and executed by the victim's browser, enabling reflected cross-site scripting on an unauthenticated endpoint.\n\nAn attacker who induces a victim to open a crafted link executes arbitrary JavaScript in the origin of the application. This permits theft of the victim's authenticated session material (the `JSESSIONID` and the non-HttpOnly `AltoroAccounts` cookie), manipulation of page content, forced state-changing requests in the victim's authenticated context, and credential/one-time-code phishing. No authentication is required to deliver the payload, and the endpoint is directly reachable over the network.\n\n1. HTML-encode the `HostName` value before it is written into the response, using the application's existing encoder `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb` (backed by `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) rather than the weaker regex-based `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`.\n2. Correct the response type: a JSON payload should be served as `application/json`, not `text/html`. This removes the browser's HTML parsing context for the response and is the appropriate fix for the endpoint's actual purpose.\n3. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (for example `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`) and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` at the application or container level as defense in depth.\n4. Apply the same server-side output encoding to all other locations that reflect user input (for example the feedback confirmation pages) and prefer the framework's implicit escaping over raw scriptlet output."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-522",
              "name": "Plaintext credential and database schema disclosure in the admin console user list",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext credential and database schema disclosure in the admin console user list"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The administrator console page `/admin/admin.jsp`, reachable by any authenticated low-privilege user, renders a user-selection `<select>` whose option values contain the plaintext `username:password:role` of every account in the `PEOPLE` table, together with database schema metadata. The disclosed passwords are the actual stored secrets: the leaked administrator credential authenticates successfully and grants access to the admin console, while the account's documented default password does not work."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The administrator console page `/admin/admin.jsp`, reachable by any authenticated low-privilege user, renders a user-selection `<select>` whose option values contain the plaintext `username:password:role` of every account in the `PEOPLE` table, together with database schema metadata. The disclosed passwords are the actual stored secrets: the leaked administrator credential authenticates successfully and grants access to the admin console, while the account's documented default password does not work.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user obtains the plaintext passwords of all user accounts exposed by the console, including a valid administrator credential. The leaked `admin` credential authenticates successfully against the application (the documented default `admin`/`admin` does not), giving the attacker control of the administrative account and the privileged functions it can reach. The response additionally discloses internal database structure (table names, including Apache Derby `SYS.*` metadata tables, and column names such as `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET`, `ROLE`, `ACCOUNT_ID`, `BALANCE`) and live account records (account identifiers, names and balances), which lowers the effort required for further authorization and injection attacks. Because the passwords are returned in cleartext, this confirms they are not stored with an adaptive hash, so any database compromise yields directly reusable credentials.\n\n1. Remove password values from every server-rendered view. The user-selection control should carry only an opaque account identifier as its option value and only a non-sensitive label (username or display name) as its text.\n2. Never serialize credential fields into HTML. Apply a strict output contract (for example a DTO/allow-list) when populating administrative dropdowns so that `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET` and other secret columns cannot reach the response, even if the underlying query selects them.\n3. Stop storing passwords recoverably. Store adaptive salted hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 or PBKDF2) and compare with a constant-time verification function. If any password was stored or transmitted in cleartext in this system, treat the credential set as compromised and rotate every affected account, including the administrator account.\n4. Restrict the administrative console to administrators. Require an explicit administrative authorization check on `/admin/*` rather than authentication alone, so exposure of an authenticated session does not equate to administrative read access.\n5. Remove schema metadata from application responses and avoid returning database catalog rows or column names to the client; log detailed database errors server-side only.\n6. Add regression coverage that asserts no response body from an administrative page contains password-like values or schema identifiers.",
                "markdown": "The administrator console page `/admin/admin.jsp`, reachable by any authenticated low-privilege user, renders a user-selection `<select>` whose option values contain the plaintext `username:password:role` of every account in the `PEOPLE` table, together with database schema metadata. The disclosed passwords are the actual stored secrets: the leaked administrator credential authenticates successfully and grants access to the admin console, while the account's documented default password does not work.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user obtains the plaintext passwords of all user accounts exposed by the console, including a valid administrator credential. The leaked `admin` credential authenticates successfully against the application (the documented default `admin`/`admin` does not), giving the attacker control of the administrative account and the privileged functions it can reach. The response additionally discloses internal database structure (table names, including Apache Derby `SYS.*` metadata tables, and column names such as `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET`, `ROLE`, `ACCOUNT_ID`, `BALANCE`) and live account records (account identifiers, names and balances), which lowers the effort required for further authorization and injection attacks. Because the passwords are returned in cleartext, this confirms they are not stored with an adaptive hash, so any database compromise yields directly reusable credentials.\n\n1. Remove password values from every server-rendered view. The user-selection control should carry only an opaque account identifier as its option value and only a non-sensitive label (username or display name) as its text.\n2. Never serialize credential fields into HTML. Apply a strict output contract (for example a DTO/allow-list) when populating administrative dropdowns so that `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET` and other secret columns cannot reach the response, even if the underlying query selects them.\n3. Stop storing passwords recoverably. Store adaptive salted hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 or PBKDF2) and compare with a constant-time verification function. If any password was stored or transmitted in cleartext in this system, treat the credential set as compromised and rotate every affected account, including the administrator account.\n4. Restrict the administrative console to administrators. Require an explicit administrative authorization check on `/admin/*` rather than authentication alone, so exposure of an authenticated session does not equate to administrative read access.\n5. Remove schema metadata from application responses and avoid returning database catalog rows or column names to the client; log detailed database errors server-side only.\n6. Add regression coverage that asserts no response body from an administrative page contains password-like values or schema identifiers."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-522",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/522.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Unauthenticated path traversal in the index.jsp `content` parameter allows reading protected WEB-INF deployment files",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated path traversal in the index.jsp `content` parameter allows reading protected WEB-INF deployment files"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `content` parameter of `/index.jsp` is used to select a file to include without validating that the resolved path stays inside the intended `/static/` content directory. An unauthenticated attacker can supply `../` sequences to escape that directory and read files that are protected by the Servlet specification (for example `/WEB-INF/web.xml`), disclosing the application's full deployment descriptor, servlet/filter classes and URL mappings."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `content` parameter of `/index.jsp` is used to select a file to include without validating that the resolved path stays inside the intended `/static/` content directory. An unauthenticated attacker can supply `../` sequences to escape that directory and read files that are protected by the Servlet specification (for example `/WEB-INF/web.xml`), disclosing the application's full deployment descriptor, servlet/filter classes and URL mappings.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can read arbitrary resources that reside inside the deployed web application, including the protected `WEB-INF` tree (`WEB-INF/web.xml`, `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`, and other server-side configuration/source artifacts shipped in the package). The leak of `web.xml` exposes the entire security configuration of the application — every filter and servlet class, their URL mappings and error pages — which directly reveals the access-control misconfiguration (`AdminFilter` bound only to the mistyped path `/adimn/*`) that lets any authenticated user reach administrative functions, and gives the internal endpoint/class inventory needed to target further attacks. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nDo not build a servlet-internal path directly from user input. Resolve the requested resource against a fixed base directory and then verify that the canonicalised result still begins with that base directory before forwarding/including it; reject the request otherwise. Because the value is resolved by the servlet container, apply the check after normalisation so that encoded traversal sequences (`..%2f`, `%2e%2e%2f`, backslashes) are also covered. Prefer serving the parameter as a fixed, server-side allow-list of known content keys mapped to resources rather than a free-form path, and keep all configuration and class resources under `WEB-INF`/`META-INF` where the container already restricts direct access.",
                "markdown": "The `content` parameter of `/index.jsp` is used to select a file to include without validating that the resolved path stays inside the intended `/static/` content directory. An unauthenticated attacker can supply `../` sequences to escape that directory and read files that are protected by the Servlet specification (for example `/WEB-INF/web.xml`), disclosing the application's full deployment descriptor, servlet/filter classes and URL mappings.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can read arbitrary resources that reside inside the deployed web application, including the protected `WEB-INF` tree (`WEB-INF/web.xml`, `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`, and other server-side configuration/source artifacts shipped in the package). The leak of `web.xml` exposes the entire security configuration of the application — every filter and servlet class, their URL mappings and error pages — which directly reveals the access-control misconfiguration (`AdminFilter` bound only to the mistyped path `/adimn/*`) that lets any authenticated user reach administrative functions, and gives the internal endpoint/class inventory needed to target further attacks. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nDo not build a servlet-internal path directly from user input. Resolve the requested resource against a fixed base directory and then verify that the canonicalised result still begins with that base directory before forwarding/including it; reject the request otherwise. Because the value is resolved by the servlet container, apply the check after normalisation so that encoded traversal sequences (`..%2f`, `%2e%2e%2f`, backslashes) are also covered. Prefer serving the parameter as a fixed, server-side allow-list of known content keys mapped to resources rather than a free-form path, and keep all configuration and class resources under `WEB-INF`/`META-INF` where the container already restricts direct access."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization exposes any customer's balance and transaction history",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization exposes any customer's balance and transaction history"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The account REST endpoints (`/api/account/{accountNo}` and `/api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`) and the web account-history view (`/bank/showAccount`, `/bank/balance.jsp`) fetch an account purely from the caller-supplied account number without verifying that the account belongs to the authenticated user. Any authenticated low-privilege user can read the balance and transaction history of arbitrary accounts, disclosing other customers' financial records."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The account REST endpoints (`/api/account/{accountNo}` and `/api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`) and the web account-history view (`/bank/showAccount`, `/bank/balance.jsp`) fetch an account purely from the caller-supplied account number without verifying that the account belongs to the authenticated user. Any authenticated low-privilege user can read the balance and transaction history of arbitrary accounts, disclosing other customers' financial records.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can enumerate the account-number space and read the balance, credit/debit history and last-10-transactions of accounts that are not their own. Using the lowest-privilege account (`jsmith`), 12 accounts outside the caller's own account list were retrieved in a single pass over a contiguous numeric range, so the entire account population is reachable by an authenticated user. This is horizontal privilege escalation with broad unauthorized disclosure of other customers' financial data.\n\nBind every account lookup to the authenticated caller before any account data is read. For the REST resource, resolve the caller via the authenticated request context and reject the request when the requested account number is not among the caller's accounts — the existing helper `User.lookupAccount(Long)` already returns `null` for accounts that do not belong to the user and should be used as the gate, returning HTTP 403 (or 404 to avoid leaking account existence). Apply the same check to every endpoint that takes an account identifier, including the last-10-transactions route. On the web side, validate the account identifier against the session user's accounts in the account-history view and redirect to the account summary when it is not owned. Prefer a single reusable authorization helper over per-handler checks so new endpoints inherit the control, and add regression tests that assert a foreign account number yields no data on each route.",
                "markdown": "The account REST endpoints (`/api/account/{accountNo}` and `/api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`) and the web account-history view (`/bank/showAccount`, `/bank/balance.jsp`) fetch an account purely from the caller-supplied account number without verifying that the account belongs to the authenticated user. Any authenticated low-privilege user can read the balance and transaction history of arbitrary accounts, disclosing other customers' financial records.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can enumerate the account-number space and read the balance, credit/debit history and last-10-transactions of accounts that are not their own. Using the lowest-privilege account (`jsmith`), 12 accounts outside the caller's own account list were retrieved in a single pass over a contiguous numeric range, so the entire account population is reachable by an authenticated user. This is horizontal privilege escalation with broad unauthorized disclosure of other customers' financial data.\n\nBind every account lookup to the authenticated caller before any account data is read. For the REST resource, resolve the caller via the authenticated request context and reject the request when the requested account number is not among the caller's accounts — the existing helper `User.lookupAccount(Long)` already returns `null` for accounts that do not belong to the user and should be used as the gate, returning HTTP 403 (or 404 to avoid leaking account existence). Apply the same check to every endpoint that takes an account identifier, including the last-10-transactions route. On the web side, validate the account identifier against the session user's accounts in the account-history view and redirect to the account summary when it is not owned. Prefer a single reusable authorization helper over per-handler checks so new endpoints inherit the control, and add regression tests that assert a foreign account number yields no data on each route."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Broken Function-Level Authorization: Any Authenticated Customer Can Invoke Admin Functions and Take Over Accounts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken Function-Level Authorization: Any Authenticated Customer Can Invoke Admin Functions and Take Over Accounts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Altoro Mutual demo banking application does not enforce the administrator role on its administrative functions. Any authenticated customer — for example `jsmith`, an ordinary account holder with no administrative link in the UI — can directly invoke the site's admin functions (`/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword`, `/admin/addAccount`) and the corresponding REST endpoints (`/api/admin/addUser`, `/api/admin/changePassword`). This was demonstrated by using a normal customer session to create a new user, to create new bank accounts, and to reset the password of a pre-existing account that the attacker does not own and then authenticate as it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Altoro Mutual demo banking application does not enforce the administrator role on its administrative functions. Any authenticated customer — for example `jsmith`, an ordinary account holder with no administrative link in the UI — can directly invoke the site's admin functions (`/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword`, `/admin/addAccount`) and the corresponding REST endpoints (`/api/admin/addUser`, `/api/admin/changePassword`). This was demonstrated by using a normal customer session to create a new user, to create new bank accounts, and to reset the password of a pre-existing account that the attacker does not own and then authenticate as it.\n\nA low-privileged, authenticated customer is able to perform administrative operations that should require the admin role, resulting in full account takeover and privilege escalation. Demonstrated consequences: (1) creating arbitrary new user accounts that can then authenticate; (2) resetting the password of any existing user, including accounts the attacker does not own — the attacker then logs in as that victim (proven against account `test_normal_abc`); and (3) creating new bank accounts attached to any user. In effect, any customer can mint credentials and seize other customers' accounts, which for a banking application equates to complete compromise of customer authentication and account integrity. The administrative console, which also discloses the full user list and database schema, becomes readable by every logged-in user.\n\n1. Correct the AdminFilter mapping in `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml`: change the URL pattern from the misspelled `/adimn/*` to `/admin/*` so the administrator-role filter actually runs for every administrative route. This is the primary defect and closes the demonstrated web-console exploit.\n2. Extend role enforcement to the REST administration surface. The URL-pattern-based AdminFilter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` must be protected separately — for example by requiring the authenticated principal's role to be admin inside `ApiAuthFilter` for the `AdminAPI` resource class, or by invoking an equivalent role check at the start of each admin handler.\n3. Do not rely on the `enableAdminFunctions` feature flag as a security control. It is a behavior toggle that is off by default and can be enabled by configuration; authorization must not depend on it.\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege at the function boundary: each administrative handler (`addUser`, `changePassword`, `addAccount` and their API equivalents) should deny by default and re-verify the caller's role on every request, rather than trusting a single upstream filter that can be mis-mapped.\n5. Add a regression test that authenticates as a known non-admin user and asserts that every `/admin/*` and `/api/admin/*` route returns an authorization failure (redirect to login/main or HTTP 403) and performs no state change.",
                "markdown": "The Altoro Mutual demo banking application does not enforce the administrator role on its administrative functions. Any authenticated customer — for example `jsmith`, an ordinary account holder with no administrative link in the UI — can directly invoke the site's admin functions (`/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword`, `/admin/addAccount`) and the corresponding REST endpoints (`/api/admin/addUser`, `/api/admin/changePassword`). This was demonstrated by using a normal customer session to create a new user, to create new bank accounts, and to reset the password of a pre-existing account that the attacker does not own and then authenticate as it.\n\nA low-privileged, authenticated customer is able to perform administrative operations that should require the admin role, resulting in full account takeover and privilege escalation. Demonstrated consequences: (1) creating arbitrary new user accounts that can then authenticate; (2) resetting the password of any existing user, including accounts the attacker does not own — the attacker then logs in as that victim (proven against account `test_normal_abc`); and (3) creating new bank accounts attached to any user. In effect, any customer can mint credentials and seize other customers' accounts, which for a banking application equates to complete compromise of customer authentication and account integrity. The administrative console, which also discloses the full user list and database schema, becomes readable by every logged-in user.\n\n1. Correct the AdminFilter mapping in `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml`: change the URL pattern from the misspelled `/adimn/*` to `/admin/*` so the administrator-role filter actually runs for every administrative route. This is the primary defect and closes the demonstrated web-console exploit.\n2. Extend role enforcement to the REST administration surface. The URL-pattern-based AdminFilter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` must be protected separately — for example by requiring the authenticated principal's role to be admin inside `ApiAuthFilter` for the `AdminAPI` resource class, or by invoking an equivalent role check at the start of each admin handler.\n3. Do not rely on the `enableAdminFunctions` feature flag as a security control. It is a behavior toggle that is off by default and can be enabled by configuration; authorization must not depend on it.\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege at the function boundary: each administrative handler (`addUser`, `changePassword`, `addAccount` and their API equivalents) should deny by default and re-verify the caller's role on every request, rather than trusting a single upstream filter that can be mis-mapped.\n5. Add a regression test that authenticates as a known non-admin user and asserts that every `/admin/*` and `/api/admin/*` route returns an authorization failure (redirect to login/main or HTTP 403) and performs no state change."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-89",
              "name": "SQL Injection Authentication Bypass in Login (POST /api/login and POST /doLogin)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL Injection Authentication Bypass in Login (POST /api/login and POST /doLogin)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Altoro Mutual login credential check builds its SQL statement by concatenating the submitted username and password directly into the query. An unauthenticated attacker can inject SQL to bypass authentication on both the REST login (`POST /api/login`) and the web login (`POST /doLogin`), authenticating as any account — including `admin` — without knowing its password. Injection is possible through either the `username`/`uid` field (`admin'-- `) or the `password`/`passw` field (`' OR '1'='1`)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Altoro Mutual login credential check builds its SQL statement by concatenating the submitted username and password directly into the query. An unauthenticated attacker can inject SQL to bypass authentication on both the REST login (`POST /api/login`) and the web login (`POST /doLogin`), authenticating as any account — including `admin` — without knowing its password. Injection is possible through either the `username`/`uid` field (`admin'-- `) or the `password`/`passw` field (`' OR '1'='1`).\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can obtain a valid session or API bearer token for any user account, including the administrator, supplying only a crafted username or password. The forged token is accepted by the account APIs and returns the victim's accounts and transaction data (demonstrated for the administrator accounts `800000`, `800001`, `800009`, `800025`). On the web login the bypass redirects to `/bank/main.jsp` and issues the administrator's account cookie, establishing a full administrative session. Because authorization is also broken at the function level, the administrative API and console surface is exposed. This defeats the application's primary authentication control.\n\nReplace all dynamic SQL construction in the authentication (and related) code paths with parameterized queries or an ORM/query builder that binds values — specifically, convert the string-concatenated query in `DBUtil.isValidUser` to a JDBC `PreparedStatement` with `?` placeholders and `setString`, so user input is always treated as data. Never concatenate user-controlled values into SQL text. Apply the same fix to other queries built from request input (for example `DBUtil.getUserInfo`, which concatenates the username into a `WHERE USER_ID = '...'` clause). As defence in depth, add centralized input validation, enforce least privilege on the database account, and return a single generic authentication-failure message so injected errors and valid-user distinctions are not observable.",
                "markdown": "The Altoro Mutual login credential check builds its SQL statement by concatenating the submitted username and password directly into the query. An unauthenticated attacker can inject SQL to bypass authentication on both the REST login (`POST /api/login`) and the web login (`POST /doLogin`), authenticating as any account — including `admin` — without knowing its password. Injection is possible through either the `username`/`uid` field (`admin'-- `) or the `password`/`passw` field (`' OR '1'='1`).\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can obtain a valid session or API bearer token for any user account, including the administrator, supplying only a crafted username or password. The forged token is accepted by the account APIs and returns the victim's accounts and transaction data (demonstrated for the administrator accounts `800000`, `800001`, `800009`, `800025`). On the web login the bypass redirects to `/bank/main.jsp` and issues the administrator's account cookie, establishing a full administrative session. Because authorization is also broken at the function level, the administrative API and console surface is exposed. This defeats the application's primary authentication control.\n\nReplace all dynamic SQL construction in the authentication (and related) code paths with parameterized queries or an ORM/query builder that binds values — specifically, convert the string-concatenated query in `DBUtil.isValidUser` to a JDBC `PreparedStatement` with `?` placeholders and `setString`, so user input is always treated as data. Never concatenate user-controlled values into SQL text. Apply the same fix to other queries built from request input (for example `DBUtil.getUserInfo`, which concatenates the username into a `WHERE USER_ID = '...'` clause). As defence in depth, add centralized input validation, enforce least privilege on the database account, and return a single generic authentication-failure message so injected errors and valid-user distinctions are not observable."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1284",
              "name": "Negative transfer amount in POST /api/transfer reverses fund flow, allowing theft from arbitrary accounts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Negative transfer amount in POST /api/transfer reverses fund flow, allowing theft from arbitrary accounts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer` does not validate the sign of `transferAmount`. A negative amount inverts the debit/credit, so a transfer \"to\" a third-party account actually removes funds from that account and credits the caller. The equivalent web endpoint (`POST /bank/doTransfer`) rejects negative amounts, so only the REST API is affected. The same endpoint also lacks any insufficient-funds or upper-bound validation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer` does not validate the sign of `transferAmount`. A negative amount inverts the debit/credit, so a transfer \"to\" a third-party account actually removes funds from that account and credits the caller. The equivalent web endpoint (`POST /bank/doTransfer`) rejects negative amounts, so only the REST API is affected. The same endpoint also lacks any insufficient-funds or upper-bound validation.\n\nAn authenticated user can move funds OUT of any third-party account by naming it as the destination and sending a negative `transferAmount`, crediting their own account. This is unauthorized modification of another customer's balance (funds theft) reachable without any special role. The absence of an amount bound additionally permits nonsensical balances and floating-point corruption of the ledger.\n\nReject any `transferAmount` that is not a finite number strictly greater than zero, and enforce a sane maximum and precision, on the server for both the REST API and the web servlet. Use a decimal/fixed-point money type rather than a binary double, and validate the parsed value (reject NaN/Infinity/overflow) before it reaches the ledger. As defence in depth, recompute the signed direction of the debit/credit from the validated positive amount rather than from a possibly-negative user value. Also enforce an insufficient-funds rule if overdrafts are not intended.",
                "markdown": "The REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer` does not validate the sign of `transferAmount`. A negative amount inverts the debit/credit, so a transfer \"to\" a third-party account actually removes funds from that account and credits the caller. The equivalent web endpoint (`POST /bank/doTransfer`) rejects negative amounts, so only the REST API is affected. The same endpoint also lacks any insufficient-funds or upper-bound validation.\n\nAn authenticated user can move funds OUT of any third-party account by naming it as the destination and sending a negative `transferAmount`, crediting their own account. This is unauthorized modification of another customer's balance (funds theft) reachable without any special role. The absence of an amount bound additionally permits nonsensical balances and floating-point corruption of the ledger.\n\nReject any `transferAmount` that is not a finite number strictly greater than zero, and enforce a sane maximum and precision, on the server for both the REST API and the web servlet. Use a decimal/fixed-point money type rather than a binary double, and validate the parsed value (reject NaN/Infinity/overflow) before it reaches the ledger. As defence in depth, recompute the signed direction of the debit/credit from the validated positive amount rather than from a possibly-negative user value. Also enforce an insufficient-funds rule if overdrafts are not intended."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1284",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1284.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-682",
              "name": "Self-transfer (fromAccount equal to toAccount) creates funds instead of being a no-op",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Self-transfer (fromAccount equal to toAccount) creates funds instead of being a no-op"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The fund-transfer operation credits the destination account but does not debit the source when both accounts are the same. Because the server does not enforce that `fromAccount` differs from `toAccount` (only the browser JavaScript does), an attacker can repeatedly \"transfer\" an amount to the same account and increase its balance without any source of funds. This affects both the web endpoint `POST /bank/doTransfer` and the REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The fund-transfer operation credits the destination account but does not debit the source when both accounts are the same. Because the server does not enforce that `fromAccount` differs from `toAccount` (only the browser JavaScript does), an attacker can repeatedly \"transfer\" an amount to the same account and increase its balance without any source of funds. This affects both the web endpoint `POST /bank/doTransfer` and the REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer`.\n\nAn authenticated user can mint unlimited funds into any account they can name as both source and destination, effectively setting the balance to an arbitrary value. This breaks conservation of value in the ledger: money is created from nothing, transactions are recorded as successful, and the resulting balance is durable and visible through all account views. It undermines the integrity of every downstream financial computation.\n\nEnforce on the server (for both the REST API and the web servlet) that `fromAccount` and `toAccount` are different accounts, rejecting the request otherwise — do not rely on the browser JavaScript check. More generally, implement the transfer as a single atomic transaction that debits the source and credits the destination using a conservation-preserving update (e.g., conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= amount` and `SET balance = balance - amount` / `balance + amount`), so a self-referential move cannot net-credit an account. Add a reconciliation/invariant check that the sum of balances is unchanged by a transfer.",
                "markdown": "The fund-transfer operation credits the destination account but does not debit the source when both accounts are the same. Because the server does not enforce that `fromAccount` differs from `toAccount` (only the browser JavaScript does), an attacker can repeatedly \"transfer\" an amount to the same account and increase its balance without any source of funds. This affects both the web endpoint `POST /bank/doTransfer` and the REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer`.\n\nAn authenticated user can mint unlimited funds into any account they can name as both source and destination, effectively setting the balance to an arbitrary value. This breaks conservation of value in the ledger: money is created from nothing, transactions are recorded as successful, and the resulting balance is durable and visible through all account views. It undermines the integrity of every downstream financial computation.\n\nEnforce on the server (for both the REST API and the web servlet) that `fromAccount` and `toAccount` are different accounts, rejecting the request otherwise — do not rely on the browser JavaScript check. More generally, implement the transfer as a single atomic transaction that debits the source and credits the destination using a conservation-preserving update (e.g., conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= amount` and `SET balance = balance - amount` / `balance + amount`), so a self-referential move cannot net-credit an account. Add a reconciliation/invariant check that the sum of balances is unchanged by a transfer."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-682",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/682.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-362",
              "name": "Race condition (non-atomic balance read-modify-write) on POST /api/transfer causes lost updates despite HTTP 200 successes",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Race condition (non-atomic balance read-modify-write) on POST /api/transfer causes lost updates despite HTTP 200 successes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Fund transfers on `POST /api/transfer` are implemented as a non-atomic read-modify-write of account balances. When multiple transfers are submitted concurrently, they overwrite each other's balance updates, so many transfers that each return HTTP 200 \"success\" are silently lost. The number of applied transfers no longer matches the number of successful responses, producing an inconsistent ledger."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Fund transfers on `POST /api/transfer` are implemented as a non-atomic read-modify-write of account balances. When multiple transfers are submitted concurrently, they overwrite each other's balance updates, so many transfers that each return HTTP 200 \"success\" are silently lost. The number of applied transfers no longer matches the number of successful responses, producing an inconsistent ledger.\n\nTransfers acknowledged as successful are silently dropped, so account balances are inconsistent with their transaction history and with the responses returned to clients. A user or automated client that issues transfers under load (including retries) can end up with a ledger that does not reflect the actions reported as completed, undermining financial-record integrity and auditability. Under some interleavings the debit and credit sides diverge slightly, which in a real system would create small reconciliation discrepancies.\n\nMake balance updates atomic and serialized. Perform each transfer in a single database transaction using atomic balance mutations (e.g. `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE account_id = ?` and `SET balance = balance + ?` inside one transaction) rather than read-compute-write, or apply row-level locking / `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the affected rows for the duration of the transfer. Where optimistic concurrency is used, enforce a version/ETag check and retry on conflict so no acknowledged update is lost. Ensure the success response is only returned after the transaction commits.",
                "markdown": "Fund transfers on `POST /api/transfer` are implemented as a non-atomic read-modify-write of account balances. When multiple transfers are submitted concurrently, they overwrite each other's balance updates, so many transfers that each return HTTP 200 \"success\" are silently lost. The number of applied transfers no longer matches the number of successful responses, producing an inconsistent ledger.\n\nTransfers acknowledged as successful are silently dropped, so account balances are inconsistent with their transaction history and with the responses returned to clients. A user or automated client that issues transfers under load (including retries) can end up with a ledger that does not reflect the actions reported as completed, undermining financial-record integrity and auditability. Under some interleavings the debit and credit sides diverge slightly, which in a real system would create small reconciliation discrepancies.\n\nMake balance updates atomic and serialized. Perform each transfer in a single database transaction using atomic balance mutations (e.g. `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE account_id = ?` and `SET balance = balance + ?` inside one transaction) rather than read-compute-write, or apply row-level locking / `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the affected rows for the duration of the transfer. Where optimistic concurrency is used, enforce a version/ETag check and retry on conflict so no acknowledged update is lost. Ensure the success response is only returned after the transaction commits."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-362",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/362.html"
            },
