      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected XSS in /util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp HostName parameter\n\nThe `HostName` query parameter of `/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp` is written directly into an HTML response with no output encoding. Because the response is served with `Content-Type: text/html`, an attacker-supplied value containing markup is rendered and executed by the victim's browser, enabling reflected cross-site scripting on an unauthenticated endpoint."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "WebContent/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4,
                  "endLine": 4,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "\"HostName\": \"<%=request.getParameter(\"HostName\")%>\","
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Encode the reflected HostName value with the application's existing HTML encoder"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "WebContent/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4,
                        "endLine": 4
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "\"HostName\": \"<%= com.ibm.security.appscan.altoromutual.util.ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb(request.getParameter(\"HostName\")) %>\","
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. HTML-encode the `HostName` value before it is written into the response, using the application's existing encoder `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb` (backed by `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) rather than the weaker regex-based `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`.\n2. Correct the response type: a JSON payload should be served as `application/json`, not `text/html`. This removes the browser's HTML parsing context for the response and is the appropriate fix for the endpoint's actual purpose.\n3. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (for example `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`) and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` at the application or container level as defense in depth.\n4. Apply the same server-side output encoding to all other locations that reflect user input (for example the feedback confirmation pages) and prefer the framework's implicit escaping over raw scriptlet output.",
                "markdown": "1. HTML-encode the `HostName` value before it is written into the response, using the application's existing encoder `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb` (backed by `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) rather than the weaker regex-based `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`.\n2. Correct the response type: a JSON payload should be served as `application/json`, not `text/html`. This removes the browser's HTML parsing context for the response and is the appropriate fix for the endpoint's actual purpose.\n3. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (for example `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`) and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` at the application or container level as defense in depth.\n4. Apply the same server-side output encoding to all other locations that reflect user input (for example the feedback confirmation pages) and prefer the framework's implicit escaping over raw scriptlet output."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "9fa8087e7da4dededac7d5238d82d977456f9d6f41d52086877063e3c7a96ebd"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:41:12 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An attacker who induces a victim to open a crafted link executes arbitrary JavaScript in the origin of the application. This permits theft of the victim's authenticated session material (the `JSESSIONID` and the non-HttpOnly `AltoroAccounts` cookie), manipulation of page content, forced state-changing requests in the victim's authenticated context, and credential/one-time-code phishing. No authentication is required to deliver the payload, and the endpoint is directly reachable over the network.",
              "technical_analysis": "The endpoint is implemented as a four-line JSP scriptlet that interpolates the raw request parameter into a JSON-shaped body and returns it as HTML. The source is:\n\n```jsp title=WebContent/util/serverStatusCheckService.jsp startLineNumber=1 endLineNumber=6\n<%@ page language=\"java\" contentType=\"text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1\" pageEncoding=\"ISO-8859-1\"%>\n\n{\n\t\"HostName\": \"<%=request.getParameter(\"HostName\")%>\",\n\t\"HostStatus\": \"OK\"\n}\n```\n\nThe scriptlet expression `<%=request.getParameter(\"HostName\")%>` emits the value verbatim; no call to the application's own encoder (`ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb`, which wraps `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) is made. The page directive sets `contentType=\"text/html\"`, so the browser parses the reflected value as HTML. A `<script>` element in the parameter therefore executes. The response carries no `Content-Security-Policy` and no `X-Content-Type-Options` header, and the reflection occurs outside any quoting that would neutralize markup.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. HTML-encode the `HostName` value before it is written into the response, using the application's existing encoder `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb` (backed by `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml`) rather than the weaker regex-based `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`.\n2. Correct the response type: a JSON payload should be served as `application/json`, not `text/html`. This removes the browser's HTML parsing context for the response and is the appropriate fix for the endpoint's actual purpose.\n3. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (for example `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`) and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` at the application or container level as defense in depth.\n4. Apply the same server-side output encoding to all other locations that reflect user input (for example the feedback confirmation pages) and prefer the framework's implicit escaping over raw scriptlet output.",
              "counterevidence": "A pre-filing control review was performed and no blocking control is present on this path: the value is not passed through any encoder, the response is served as `text/html`, and neither `Content-Security-Policy` nor `X-Content-Type-Options` is returned. Execution was confirmed in a real browser engine rather than inferred from source alone, and a benign value (a plain hostname) is reflected in the same position, confirming the reflection point is the injected context and not a dead/broken endpoint. The application ships an HTML encoder (`ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb`) but it is not invoked here, so the existence of that helper does not neutralize this sink. The only residual doubt at the exploitation level is the need for victim link interaction (a standard reflected-XSS precondition), which is reflected in the CVSS user-interaction metric rather than treated as a blocker.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the endpoint were changed to serve `application/json` (so browsers no longer parse it as HTML) or a `Content-Security-Policy` blocking inline script were applied, exploitability would drop to informational; conversely, if analysis showed the reflected value were persisted and shown to other users, this would escalate to stored XSS and a higher severity.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: the patched line passes the reflected value through `ServletUtil.sanitizeWeb`, which is `StringEscapeUtils.escapeHtml(data)` (defined in `src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/util/ServletUtil.java`, line 245). That converts `<`, `>`, `\"`, and `&` to entities, so the `<script>` element in the input is delivered as inert text and cannot open a script context; the source-to-sink reflection is therefore closed.\n\nBypass review: I checked the sibling reflection points that handle the same class of input. `WebContent/feedbacksuccess.jsp` and `WebContent/index.jsp` already route values through `ServletUtil.sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`, whereas this JSP applies no filter at all, so this is the unguarded instance rather than a safe sibling. I confirmed the fix targets the raw scriptlet output specifically. I used `sanitizeWeb` (proper entity encoding) rather than the regex blacklist `sanitzieHtmlWithRegex`, because the regex approach is trivially bypassable (alternate encodings, unlisted handlers) and would not constitute a complete fix.\n\nPreserved behavior: the endpoint's contract is unchanged for legitimate input — a normal hostname such as `127.0.0.1` contains no escapable characters and is echoed byte-for-byte, so the JSON shape, the `HostStatus` field, and the existing client-side JavaScript that reads `HostName` continue to work. Only values containing HTML metacharacters change, which is the intended effect.\n\nRepository checks: not executed. This is a Java/JSP web application whose build/deploy pipeline is not available in the assessment environment and the finding was confirmed against the remote deployed instance, so I could not compile or redeploy the patched JSP to re-run the proof of concept. This gate is a gap: the patch was validated by tracing the encoder's behavior and the JSP expression syntax, not by rebuilding and re-testing the application. The one-line change is limited to a single scriptlet expression and introduces no new imports or symbols beyond an already-defined public static method.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Craft a URL that injects a script element into the `HostName` parameter.\n2. Send the request (or deliver the URL to a victim) and observe that the value is reflected verbatim in the response body, which is served as `text/html`.\n3. In a browser, the injected script executes in the application origin: a proof payload that sets `window.__xss_pwned = 1337` and rewrites `document.title` confirms execution.\n4. Confirm by reading back `window.__xss_pwned` and `document.title` from the rendered page.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Plaintext credential and database schema disclosure in the admin console user list\n\nThe administrator console page `/admin/admin.jsp`, reachable by any authenticated low-privilege user, renders a user-selection `<select>` whose option values contain the plaintext `username:password:role` of every account in the `PEOPLE` table, together with database schema metadata. The disclosed passwords are the actual stored secrets: the leaked administrator credential authenticates successfully and grants access to the admin console, while the account's documented default password does not work."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/admin/admin.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "5ef21d9fbbea11eb1ff24b7e060b20584c75df69245c6be59271b6b4a9a12b69"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "486125e8b99b6f1b21594686d4656a69931c4cf3f81988cae7e34b18a53aa672",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:44:56 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/admin/admin.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-522",
              "impact": "An authenticated low-privilege user obtains the plaintext passwords of all user accounts exposed by the console, including a valid administrator credential. The leaked `admin` credential authenticates successfully against the application (the documented default `admin`/`admin` does not), giving the attacker control of the administrative account and the privileged functions it can reach. The response additionally discloses internal database structure (table names, including Apache Derby `SYS.*` metadata tables, and column names such as `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET`, `ROLE`, `ACCOUNT_ID`, `BALANCE`) and live account records (account identifiers, names and balances), which lowers the effort required for further authorization and injection attacks. Because the passwords are returned in cleartext, this confirms they are not stored with an adaptive hash, so any database compromise yields directly reusable credentials.",
              "technical_analysis": "`GET /admin/admin.jsp` builds a user dropdown by iterating over the application's user records and writing each record into an `<option>` element. Instead of emitting only the account identifier, the rendered option value concatenates the user identifier, the plaintext password and the role (for example `pdata1admin:admin_hacked:admin` and `pd2_13jsmith:demo1234:user`). The same response also emits database metadata as options: table names (`tbl2PEOPLE`, `tbl0ACCOUNTS`, `tbl25TRANSACTIONS`, `tbl1FEEDBACK`, plus Derby `SYS.*` catalog tables) and column names for the `PEOPLE` table (`pcol0FIRST_NAME`, `pcol3PASSWORD`, `pcol4ROLE`, `pcol5TOTP_SECRET`, `pcol6USER_ID`) and the `ACCOUNTS` table (`acol0ACCOUNT_ID`, `acol2BALANCE`, `acol3USERID`).\n\nTwo properties make the exposure severe. First, the leaked password values are the real secrets rather than labels: the `admin` password `admin_hacked` authenticates successfully, and the `jsmith` value `demo1234` matches the account's known working password. Second, the affected page requires only an authenticated session, not an administrative role: the request was performed with a `jsmith` session, so any user who can log in can harvest every credential. The credential list also tracks live database state — it contains accounts created during the current test window (for example `hackerman`, `hacktest456`, `jsmith_hack`, `i019_test_1783661487`, `adduser_baseline`), and a newly created account appeared in the list immediately after creation, confirming the values are read from the database at request time rather than being a static demo artifact.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Remove password values from every server-rendered view. The user-selection control should carry only an opaque account identifier as its option value and only a non-sensitive label (username or display name) as its text.\n2. Never serialize credential fields into HTML. Apply a strict output contract (for example a DTO/allow-list) when populating administrative dropdowns so that `PASSWORD`, `TOTP_SECRET` and other secret columns cannot reach the response, even if the underlying query selects them.\n3. Stop storing passwords recoverably. Store adaptive salted hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2 or PBKDF2) and compare with a constant-time verification function. If any password was stored or transmitted in cleartext in this system, treat the credential set as compromised and rotate every affected account, including the administrator account.\n4. Restrict the administrative console to administrators. Require an explicit administrative authorization check on `/admin/*` rather than authentication alone, so exposure of an authenticated session does not equate to administrative read access.\n5. Remove schema metadata from application responses and avoid returning database catalog rows or column names to the client; log detailed database errors server-side only.\n6. Add regression coverage that asserts no response body from an administrative page contains password-like values or schema identifiers.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest argument against this finding is that the target is a public, intentionally vulnerable demo application whose data is synthetic, so no real customer financial data is exposed and the business impact is limited; this is reflected in the Medium rating. A second argument is that the page requires an authenticated session, which is why the vector uses `PR:L` rather than `PR:N`. A third is that a separate authorization defect already allows any logged-in user to invoke administrative functions, so obtaining the `admin` credential adds less marginal privilege than it would on a hardened deployment; that weakness was confirmed independently (both the `jsmith` session and the `admin` session return HTTP 200 from `/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/feedbackreview.jsp` and accept `POST /admin/addUser`).\n\nPlaceholders or static demo strings were actively ruled out. The leaked `admin` password `admin_hacked` authenticates successfully, whereas the account's documented default password `admin` is rejected — a negative control showing the leaked value is the genuine, non-default secret. The leaked `jsmith` value `demo1234` matches the account's independently verified working password. The credential set changes with database state: it contains accounts created during the current test window, and a newly created account appeared in the dropdown immediately afterwards. Finally, the API authorization token returned by `/api/login` decodes to `admin:admin_hacked`, confirming the value is used by the authentication logic and is not a display artifact.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the same administrator password (or the other disclosed passwords) is reused on a production or non-demo system, or proof that the page is reachable without any authentication, would raise the severity; evidence that the exposed accounts are confined to a synthetic demo instance with no downstream reuse keeps it at the current level.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate to the application with a low-privilege account by submitting `uid` and `passw` to `POST /doLogin`; a successful login redirects to `/bank/main.jsp` and sets a `JSESSIONID` cookie.\n2. Request `GET /admin/admin.jsp` with that session and confirm the response is HTTP 200.\n3. Extract every `<option value=\"...\">` whose value has the form `username:password:role`. The extracted set includes the administrative account `admin` with password `admin_hacked` (role `admin`).\n4. Authenticate to the application with the leaked `admin` / `admin_hacked` credential and confirm the login is accepted and `/admin/admin.jsp` is reachable.\n5. Compare against the account's documented default password: `admin` / `admin` is rejected, confirming the leaked value is the genuine stored secret. All requests are read-only; the final step only authenticates.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated path traversal in the index.jsp `content` parameter allows reading protected WEB-INF deployment files\n\nThe `content` parameter of `/index.jsp` is used to select a file to include without validating that the resolved path stays inside the intended `/static/` content directory. An unauthenticated attacker can supply `../` sequences to escape that directory and read files that are protected by the Servlet specification (for example `/WEB-INF/web.xml`), disclosing the application's full deployment descriptor, servlet/filter classes and URL mappings."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/index.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b4e89d4b37e5d7e942566922782e39a75d7a5f5ee4499cfa52d343200993c2cc"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6c58275b746a212b66e13227aff805df9d5241b3a0ee6715ddb4941ae4556cfd",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:45:12 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/index.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-22",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated remote attacker can read arbitrary resources that reside inside the deployed web application, including the protected `WEB-INF` tree (`WEB-INF/web.xml`, `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`, and other server-side configuration/source artifacts shipped in the package). The leak of `web.xml` exposes the entire security configuration of the application — every filter and servlet class, their URL mappings and error pages — which directly reveals the access-control misconfiguration (`AdminFilter` bound only to the mistyped path `/adimn/*`) that lets any authenticated user reach administrative functions, and gives the internal endpoint/class inventory needed to target further attacks. No authentication or user interaction is required.",
              "technical_analysis": "The main page `index.jsp` renders a body selected by the `content` request parameter. Internally the value is turned into a context-relative path prefixed with the public content directory and forwarded/included through a `RequestDispatcher` (observable because error responses are emitted as `Failed due to The requested resource (/static/<input>) is not available`). Because the value is not canonicalised against the base directory, `../` segments traverse out of `/static/`.\n\n`GET /index.jsp?content=../WEB-INF/web.xml` resolves to `/static/../WEB-INF/web.xml` → `/WEB-INF/web.xml` and includes the raw deployment descriptor, which the Servlet specification requires to be inaccessible to clients. That the returned bytes are genuine file content (and not a template or a static page) is confirmed by comparing three behaviours on the same endpoint:\n\n- `content=../WEB-INF/web.xml` → HTTP 200, 14141 bytes, body contains the real `<web-app ...> … </web-app>` descriptor including `AuthFilter`/`AdminFilter` classes and the mistyped `/adimn/*` mapping.\n- `content=WEB-INF/web.xml` (no traversal) → the requested path is reported as `not available`, proving the app does not serve `WEB-INF` by design and that the `../` is what enables the read.\n- `content=../WEB-INF/does-not-exist.xml` → `not available`, proving the parameter resolves real files rather than emitting a fixed template.\n\nURL- and separator-encoded payloads (`..%2f`, `%2e%2e%2f`, `..%5c`) return the same 14141-byte content, indicating the value is decoded before path resolution. Deeper traversal (`../../WEB-INF/web.xml`) raises a `NullPointerException` (`org.apache.jasper.JasperException`) because Tomcat clamps the normalised path at the web application root, so the read is bounded to the deployed application (web root plus `WEB-INF`/`META-INF`) and does not reach the host OS filesystem.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not build a servlet-internal path directly from user input. Resolve the requested resource against a fixed base directory and then verify that the canonicalised result still begins with that base directory before forwarding/including it; reject the request otherwise. Because the value is resolved by the servlet container, apply the check after normalisation so that encoded traversal sequences (`..%2f`, `%2e%2e%2f`, backslashes) are also covered. Prefer serving the parameter as a fixed, server-side allow-list of known content keys mapped to resources rather than a free-form path, and keep all configuration and class resources under `WEB-INF`/`META-INF` where the container already restricts direct access.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively tested alternate explanations and found none that hold. (1) \"It is just echoing a template / a fixed page\" — disproved: the controls `content=WEB-INF/web.xml` and `content=../WEB-INF/does-not-exist.xml` both return `not available`, and the traversal response carries the app's real `<web-app>` descriptor with unique servlet/filter classes and `</web-app>` terminator (7380 bytes of live file content). (2) \"Only whitelisted filenames are served\" — disproved: two distinct protected files were read (`WEB-INF/web.xml`, `META-INF/MANIFEST.MF`) and the error text echoes the attacker-supplied resolved path, showing the parameter drives path resolution. (3) \"It escapes the whole server, so it must reach OS secrets\" — on the contrary, deeper traversal (`../../…`) raises a `NullPointerException` because the container clamps the path at the web application root; the read is therefore confined to the deployed application, which limits (rather than invalidates) severity. (4) \"Authentication is required\" — disproved: reproduced with no cookies and a freshly issued session. No WAF, input filter, or allow-list was observed on this path. The remaining uncertainty is only how much sensitive material any particular deployment ships under `WEB-INF`; the primitive itself is fully confirmed.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the traversal were demonstrated to reach files outside the web application root (host `/etc/passwd`, Tomcat `conf/tomcat-users.xml`, or credential-bearing database configuration), the impact would rise to critical; conversely, if the deployment were configured to deny `RequestDispatcher` access to `WEB-INF`/`META-INF` or the `content` value were constrained to an allow-list, the finding would drop to low.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. From an unauthenticated client (no cookies/session), request `GET http://demo.testfire.net/index.jsp?content=../WEB-INF/web.xml`.\n2. Observe HTTP 200 with `Content-Length: 14141`; the response body embeds the application's real deployment descriptor (`<web-app …>` through `</web-app>`).\n3. Confirm this is real file content and not a template by comparing controls on the same endpoint: `content=WEB-INF/web.xml` (no traversal) and `content=../WEB-INF/does-not-exist.xml` both return `Failed due to The requested resource (…) is not available`.\n4. Confirm encoding bypasses by requesting `content=..%2fWEB-INF/web.xml`, `content=%2e%2e%2fWEB-INF/web.xml` and `content=..%5cWEB-INF/web.xml`, all of which return the same descriptor.\n5. Read the leaked descriptor to extract filter/servlet mappings, including `AdminFilter` bound to the mistyped `/adimn/*` path.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "IDOR / Broken Object-Level Authorization exposes any customer's balance and transaction history\n\nThe account REST endpoints (`/api/account/{accountNo}` and `/api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`) and the web account-history view (`/bank/showAccount`, `/bank/balance.jsp`) fetch an account purely from the caller-supplied account number without verifying that the account belongs to the authenticated user. Any authenticated low-privilege user can read the balance and transaction history of arbitrary accounts, disclosing other customers' financial records."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/api/AccountAPI.java"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 63,
                  "endLine": 66
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce account ownership in GET /api/account/{accountNo}"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/api/AccountAPI.java"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 116,
                  "endLine": 119
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce account ownership in GET /api/account/{accountNo}/transactions"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "WebContent/bank/balance.jsp"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 40,
                  "endLine": 41
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Reject foreign accounts in the web account-history view"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/account/{accountNo}",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/api/AccountAPI.java"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 63,
                        "endLine": 66
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// Restrict access to accounts owned by the authenticated user\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\t// Get the account balance\n\t\t\tUser user = OperationsUtil.getUser(request);\n\t\t\tAccount account = user.lookupAccount(Long.parseLong(accountNo));\n\t\t\tif (account == null) {\n\t\t\t\treturn Response.status(Response.Status.FORBIDDEN)\n\t\t\t\t\t\t.entity(\"{Error : Account not found}\")\n\t\t\t\t\t\t.build();\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdouble dblBalance = account.getBalance();"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/api/AccountAPI.java"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 116,
                        "endLine": 119
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "// Restrict access to accounts owned by the authenticated user\n\t\ttry {\n\t\t\tUser user = OperationsUtil.getUser(request);\n\t\t\tif (user.lookupAccount(Long.parseLong(accountNo)) == null) {\n\t\t\t\treturn Response.status(Response.Status.FORBIDDEN)\n\t\t\t\t\t\t.entity(\"{Error : Account not found}\")\n\t\t\t\t\t\t.build();\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t} catch (Exception e) {\n\t\t\treturn Response.status(Response.Status.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)\n\t\t\t\t\t.entity(\"{Error : \" + e.getLocalizedMessage() + \"}\")\n\t\t\t\t\t.build();\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tresponse = \"{\";\n\t\t// Get the last 10 transactions\n\t\tString last10Transactions;\n\t\tlast10Transactions = this.getLastTenTransactions(accountNo);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "WebContent/bank/balance.jsp"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 40,
                        "endLine": 41
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "java.lang.String paramName = request.getParameter(\"acctId\");\n\t\t\t\t\tString accountName = paramName;\n\t\t\t\t\tboolean accountOwned = false;\n\t\t\t\t\tif (paramName != null) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tfor (Account owned : user.getAccounts()) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tif (String.valueOf(owned.getAccountId()).equals(paramName)) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\taccountOwned = true;\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tbreak;\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t\tif (!accountOwned) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tresponse.sendRedirect(request.getContextPath() + \"/bank/main.jsp\");\n\t\t\t\t\t\treturn;\n\t\t\t\t\t}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Bind every account lookup to the authenticated caller before any account data is read. For the REST resource, resolve the caller via the authenticated request context and reject the request when the requested account number is not among the caller's accounts — the existing helper `User.lookupAccount(Long)` already returns `null` for accounts that do not belong to the user and should be used as the gate, returning HTTP 403 (or 404 to avoid leaking account existence). Apply the same check to every endpoint that takes an account identifier, including the last-10-transactions route. On the web side, validate the account identifier against the session user's accounts in the account-history view and redirect to the account summary when it is not owned. Prefer a single reusable authorization helper over per-handler checks so new endpoints inherit the control, and add regression tests that assert a foreign account number yields no data on each route.",
                "markdown": "Bind every account lookup to the authenticated caller before any account data is read. For the REST resource, resolve the caller via the authenticated request context and reject the request when the requested account number is not among the caller's accounts — the existing helper `User.lookupAccount(Long)` already returns `null` for accounts that do not belong to the user and should be used as the gate, returning HTTP 403 (or 404 to avoid leaking account existence). Apply the same check to every endpoint that takes an account identifier, including the last-10-transactions route. On the web side, validate the account identifier against the session user's accounts in the account-history view and redirect to the account summary when it is not owned. Prefer a single reusable authorization helper over per-handler checks so new endpoints inherit the control, and add regression tests that assert a foreign account number yields no data on each route."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "08ea264c79d7a9c9380905b511677efc89bf2263bd35aef9a376f22fe2f1cf8f"
