        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/arbitrary-file-write",
          "ruleIndex": 16,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Arbitrary file write — Ruled out: POST /sendFeedback (cfile hidden param)\n\nNamed control at a specific location: FeedbackServlet.doPost (src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/servlet/FeedbackServlet.java:47-67) reads ONLY name, email_addr, subject, comments - it never calls request.getParameter(\"cfile\"). Feedback is persisted via OperationsUtil.sendFeedback -> DBUtil.storeFeedback (SQL INSERT INTO FEEDBACK, DBUtil.java:520-529); no path/file-write sink exists on this route. `cfile` occurs solely as a dead hidden input in WebContent/feedback.jsp:50 (grep over src/ and WebContent/ returns no other reference). No multipart/upload handling anywhere (no @MultipartConfig / getPart / ServletFileUpload / FileOutputStream in JSPs or servlets). Live confirmation on http://demo.testfire.net: 11 distinct cfile values (comments.txt, probe_up.txt, ../WebContent/..., ../WEB-INF/..., ../../..., /tmp/..., probe_up.jsp variants) all produced a byte-identical 200 \"Thank You\" (7029 bytes); the unique marker was unreadable at /probe_up.txt, /probe_up.jsp, /comments.txt (404) and via /index.jsp?content=../<file> traversal. Prior \"needs_follow_up\" was an artifact of testing a parameter the server ignores. NOT arbitrary file write."
          },
