        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/business-logic-field-parameter-manipulation",
          "ruleIndex": 18,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Business logic – field/parameter manipulation — Ruled out: /bank/ccApply\n\nSpecific control: CCApplyServlet (src/com/ibm/security/appscan/altoromutual/servlet/CCApplyServlet.java) reads exactly ONE request parameter, `passwd` (line 47); it binds no other field, stores nothing and changes no account/credit state (\"do nothing with credit card application\"). Field injection / mass assignment is therefore impossible — any role/balance/approval-style parameter is silently ignored. Verified live with a jsmith session: GET /bank/ccApply -> 405 (POST-only, explains the earlier GET 405); POST with no passwd -> 500 (NPE at passwd.trim(), explains the earlier generic-POST 500); POST passwd=demo1234 -> 200 /bank/applysuccess.jsp (\"Your new Altoro Mutual Gold VISA\"); POST passwd=wrong -> 200 /bank/apply.jsp (\"Login Failed...\"). The only sink is passwd -> DBUtil.isValidUser(username, passwd.trim().toLowerCase()), the unparameterized credential query already reported as vuln-0009. Even a successful injection there merely yields applysuccess.jsp with no state change, so it does not constitute unauthorized field binding or business-logic abuse and is not separately reportable."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
